\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - CORRECTED-GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian stocks fall on firm yen, oil hits.   \b0\par\par\uc2 959 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 09:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     In SINGAPORE story headlined "GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian stocks\par  fall on firm yen, oil hits $35" please read in 17th paragraph\par  .... The dollar was at $1.2760  per euro at 0235 GMT,\par  down from $1.2751 in late U.S. trade but above record lows of\par  $1.2899 per euro hit in New York before Trichet's comments....\par  instead of ....The dollar was at $1.2760  per euro at\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT, up from $1.2751 in late U.S. trade but below record lows of $1.2899 hit in New York before Trichet's comments (corrects direction of euro move).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 A corrected story follows.   \par \par \uc2 By Ramthan Hussain   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 13 (Reuters) - Tokyo stocks stumbled on Tuesday on the yen's sustained strength, leading most Asian markets lower despite U.S. technology shares rising to 2-1/2-year peaks.   \par \par \uc2 Oil prices jumped to $35 a barrel, their highest since the Iraq war, on fears of another bout of icy weather in the United States that had slashed U.S crude stocks to their lowest in almost 30 years.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady against the euro on European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) concerns about excessive movement in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and held firm on the yen after comments by a Japanese official pointed at more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Financial bookmakers in London expected Britain's FTSE 100, Germany's DAX index and France's CAC-40 to open up to 0.4 percent higher.   \par \par \uc2 An MSCI index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan shed 0.6 percent, but was still near its highest level in almost four years.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's benchmark Nikkei average, which was closed on Monday, ended down 1.1 percent at 10,849.6, but was about four percent above the 25-day moving average of 10,417.04.   \par \par \uc2 "There are some overheating signals that the Nikkei has gone too far," said Fumiyuki Nakanishi, chief strategist at SMBC Friend Securities.   \par \par \uc2 High-tech stocks, which had driven the Nikkei's recent rally, pushed the market lower, led by a 2.7 percent fall in Canon Inc. NEC Corp was down more than two percent and Toshiba Corp lost 2.4 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Worries about the firm yen hit exporters such as Toyota Motor Corp, which fell 1.6 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week wasn't enough to put an end to worries about a stronger yen," said Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi Securities.   \par \par \uc2 Others viewed this as a minor setback. "The Nikkei faces a bump in the road at about 11,000, but I expect we'll rise above that point later in the week after this brief correction," said Koichi Seki, manager at Chuo Securities' equities sales department.   \par \par \uc2 Chicken-related shares fell, while seafood and pork-related shares rose after Japanese officials confirmed on Monday an outbreak of highly contagious bird flu.   \par \par \uc2 RESPITE FOR DOLLAR   \par \par \uc2 The tech-driven Nasdaq rose 1.2 percent to 2,111.8 on Wall Street, its highest close since July 2001, and the Philadelphia Stock Exchange's semiconductor index jumped three percent to 560.6, a level not seen since April 2002, as investors eyed positive corporate earnings.   \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average edged up just 0.3 percent after blue-chip companies Merck & Co and Alcoa Inc were downgraded by brokerages.   \par \par \uc2 Traders welcomed ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet's remarks to central bankers in Basel, Switzerland, on Monday that "excess volatility and brutal moves were not welcome and not appropriate" for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But they also wondered how long the reprieve would last for the battered dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "The euro has been rising continually for weeks, so Trichet's comments were a trigger for at least some pause," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinsei Bank. "The market showed a bigger reaction than you might have thought.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at $1.2760 per euro at 0235 GMT, down from $1.2751 in late U.S. trade but above record lows of $1.2899 per euro hit in New York before Trichet's comments.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar held firm around 106.5 yen after comments from Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, that authorities would take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as needed.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond (JGB) yields fell to three-week lows after a jump in U.S. Treasury prices last week on a weak jobs report and on a weaker Nikkei. But JGB price gains were curbed by prospects of a continued economic recovery in Japan.   \par \par \uc2 NYMEX February crude rose 28 cents to $35, the highest price since March 18, ahead of the contract's options expiry on Wednesday, prompting an appeal for stability from the Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries.   \par \par \uc2 Gold dipped on a firmer dollar against the euro, but the softer prices prompted fresh buying interest, sending them to hover around $426.40 from around $424.25 in early Asian trade.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong stocks dropped 0.9 percent, hit by losses in Chinese oil and petrochemical companies after Britain's BP Plc, the world's third-largest oil group, sold its two percent stake in China's biggest oil firm PetroChina.   \par \par \uc2 PetroChina fell 3.7 percent. Sinopec, Asia's largest refiner, saw a steeper decline and Sinopec Zhenhai Refining & Chemical Co (1128.HK) was also hit by fears their strategic investors might also take profits on their investments.   \par \par \uc2 South Korea's composite index shed 0.3 percent on profit-taking after its recent rally to 20-month peaks, while Taiwan stocks fell 0.2 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore stocks shed early gains of up to one percent to trade up 0.4 percent in the afternoon, while Australian shares reversed initial gains.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040113e01d0020x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
