\par\par \b \uc2 Asian stocks fall on firm yen, oil hits $35.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ramthan Hussain   \par \uc2 826 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 08:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 13 (Reuters) - Tokyo stocks stumbled on Tuesday on the yen's sustained strength, leading most Asian markets lower despite U.S. technology shares rising to 2-1/2-year peaks.    \par \par \uc2  Oil prices jumped to $35 a barrel, their highest since the Iraq war, on fears of another bout of icy weather in the United States that had slashed U.S crude stocks to their lowest in almost 30 years.    \par \par \uc2  The dollar was steady against the euro on European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) concerns about excessive movement in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and held firm on the yen after comments by a Japanese official pointed at more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Financial bookmakers in London expected Britain's FTSE 100 , Germany's\par DAX index  and France's CAC-40         to open up to 0.4 percent\par higher.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An MSCI index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan  shed 0.6 percent,\par but was still near its highest level in almost four years.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's benchmark Nikkei average , which was closed on Monday, ended\par down 1.1 percent at 10,849.6, but was about four percent above the\par 25-day moving average of 10,417.04.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There are some overheating signals that the Nikkei has gone too far,"\par said Fumiyuki Nakanishi, chief strategist at SMBC Friend Securities.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par High-tech stocks, which had driven the Nikkei's recent rally, pushed\par the market lower, led by a 2.7 percent fall in Canon Inc (7751.T). NEC\par Corp (6701.T) was down more than two percent and Toshiba Corp (6502.T)\par lost 2.4 percent.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Worries about the firm yen hit exporters such as Toyota Motor Corp\par (7203.T), which fell 1.6 percent.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week wasn't enough to put an\par end to worries about a stronger yen," said Norihiro Fujito, senior\par investment strategist at Mitsubishi Securities.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Others viewed this as a minor setback. "The Nikkei faces a bump in the\par road at about 11,000, but I expect we'll rise above that point later in\par the week after this brief correction," said Koichi Seki, manager at\par Chuo Securities' equities sales department.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Chicken-related shares fell, while seafood and pork-related shares rose\par after Japanese officials confirmed on Monday an outbreak of highly\par contagious bird flu.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The tech-driven Nasdaq  rose 1.2 percent to 2,111.8 on Wall Street, its\par highest close since July 2001, and the Philadelphia Stock Exchange's\par semiconductor index         jumped three percent to 560.6, a level not\par seen since April 2002, as investors eyed positive corporate earnings.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Dow Jones Industrial Average  edged up just 0.3 percent after\par blue-chip companies Merck & Co (MRK.N) and Alcoa Inc (AA.N) were\par downgraded by brokerages.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders welcomed ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet's remarks to central\par bankers in Basel, Switzerland, on Monday that "excess volatility and\par brutal moves were not welcome and not appropriate" for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But\par they also wondered how long the reprieve would last for the battered\par dollar.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The euro has been rising continually for weeks, so Trichet's comments\par were a trigger for at least some pause," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Shinsei Bank. "The market showed a bigger\par reaction than you might have thought.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was at $1.2760  per euro at 0235 GMT up from $1.2751 in late\par U.S. trade but below record lows of $1.2899 hit in New York before\par Trichet's comments.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar held firm around 106.5 yen  after comments from Zembei\par Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, that\par authorities would take action in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as needed.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese government bond (JGB) yields fell to three-week lows after a\par jump in U.S. Treasury prices last week on a weak jobs report and on a\par weaker Nikkei. But JGB price gains were curbed by prospects of a\par continued economic recovery in Japan.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NYMEX February crude  rose 28 cents to $35, the highest price since\par March 18, ahead of the contract's options expiry on Wednesday,\par prompting an appeal for stability from the Organisation of the\par Petroleum Exporting Countries.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Gold  dipped on a firmer dollar against the euro, but the softer prices\par prompted fresh buying interest, sending them to hover around $426.40\par from around $424.25 in early Asian trade.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hong Kong stocks  dropped 0.9 percent, hit by losses in Chinese oil and\par petrochemical companies after Britain's BP Plc (BP.L), the world's\par third-largest oil group, sold its two percent stake in China's biggest\par oil firm PetroChina (0857.HK).\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par South Korea's composite index  shed 0.3 percent on profit-taking after\par its recent rally to 20-month peaks, while Taiwan stocks         fell\par 0.2 percent.\par Singapore stocks  shed early gains of up to one percent to trade up 0.4\par percent in the afternoon, while Australian shares         reversed\par initial gains.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040113e01d001mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
