\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Tokyo stocks fall on strong yen, others gain.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ramthan Hussain  \par \uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 07:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 13 (Reuters) - Shares in Japanese electronics exporters fell on Tuesday, hit by the yen's sustained strength, but most Asian stock markets kept afloat after technology shares rose to 2-1/2-year highs on Wall Street.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices hovered at post-Iraq-war highs near $35 a barrel on fears of another bout of icy weather in the United States that had slashed U.S crude stocks to their lowest in almost 30 years.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up against the euro on European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) concerns about excessive movement in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the U.S. unit held firm on the yen after comments by a Japanese official pointed at more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 An MSCI index of Asia-Pacific shares outside \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was flat around its highest level in nearly four years.  \par \par \uc2 Electronics giant Hitachi Ltd (6501.T) fell two percent after rising to an 18-month high on Friday, and rival Toshiba Corp (6502.T) lost three percent.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week wasn't enough to put an end to worries about a stronger yen," said Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei 225 average fell one percent to 10,855.5, after closing at an 11-week high on Friday. Japanese markets were closed on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "The Nikkei faces a bump in the road at about 11,000, but I expect we'll rise above that point later in the week after this brief correction," said Koichi Seki, manager at Chuo Securities' equities sales department. The Nikkei has gained 1.7 percent so far this year.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The tech-driven Nasdaq  rose 1.2 percent to 2,111.8 on Wall Street, its\par highest close since July 2001, and the Philadelphia Stock Exchange's\par semiconductor index         jumped three percent to 560.6, a level not\par seen since April 2002, as investors eyed positive corporate earnings.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average made a slimmer 0.3 percent gain after blue-chip companies Merck & Co (MRK.N) and Alcoa Inc (AA.N) were downgraded by brokerages.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RESPITE FOR DOLLAR  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders welcomed ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet's remarks to central bankers in Basel, Switzerland, on Monday that "excess volatility and brutal moves were not welcome and not appropriate" for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But they also wondered how long the reprieve would last for the battered dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The euro has been rising continually for weeks, so Trichet's comments were a trigger for at least some pause," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Shinsei Bank. "The market showed a bigger reaction than you might have thought.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was at $1.2744 per euro at 0230 GMT, up from $1.2751 in late U.S. trade and from record lows of $1.2899 hit in New York before Trichet's comments.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar held firm around 106.5 yen following comments from Zembei Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, that authorities would take action in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as needed, and that overshooting of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates were inappropriate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond prices rose after U.S. Treasuries soared on a weak U.S. jobs report last week. But the prospect of a continued recovery in the Japanese economy prompted some investors to take profits, capping the rally.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NYMEX February crude rose 23 cents to $34.95 a barrel ahead of the contract's options expiry on Wednesday and touched $35.00, the highest price since March 18.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices were pinned down by a stronger dollar against the euro and were around $424.25 an ounce at 0220 GMT.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong stocks rose 0.5 percent but the gains were curbed by a fall in energy stocks after a large sale of PetroChina Ltd (0857.HK) shares by oil giant BP (BP.L).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's composite index shed 0.3 percent on profit-taking after the market's recent rally to 20-month peaks, while Taiwan stocks gained 0.1 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore stocks  rose almost one percent, led by tech and media\par stocks, while a rebound in BHP Billiton (BHP.AX) and strength in banks\par kept Australian shares         above water.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040113e01d000mk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
