\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steadies after warnings on euro strength.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 22:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The dollar steadied against the euro on Tuesday, recovering from Monday's record low after European politicians expressed concern about the impact a soaring euro may have on economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, after a warning shot from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, traded near flat against the dollar after notching a lifetime high a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan also chimed in. He noted that the dollar has fallen broadly against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Yet inflation, the typical symptom of a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , appears quiescent," the Fed chief said at Germany's Bundesbank in Berlin.  \par \par \uc2 "There is nothing (in Greenspan's speech) suggesting that anything is imminent in terms of policy coordination, including going into the G7 on February 7," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley & Co. in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "There is an awareness that the Europeans have put on the agenda the issue about what they believe (to be) too rapid a rise of the euro, so markets are now alert to the potential of policy response through direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or through a rate cut," Stewart said.  \par \par \uc2 Global policy-makers of the Group of Seven industrialized nations are to meet in Florida on Feb. 6-7.  \par \par \uc2 German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder, German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement and French Finance Minister Francis Mer all expressed concern about the likely impact on European economic growth of a 40 percent rise in the euro in the past two years.  \par \par \uc2 Mer said on Tuesday there would be efforts at the G7 finance ministers meeting to send "the correct signal" to financial markets on the risks of the current trend. Those comments caused a light sell-off of the euro.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar steadied around $1.2743  against the euro in afternoon New\par York trade. Against the yen       , the dollar was down 0.43 percent on\par the day to 106.23 yen, confined to a narrow trading range by market\par nervousness that Japanese authorities could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again to curb yen\par gains.\par Against the Swiss franc  the dollar was at 1.2227 francs, down about\par 0.44 percent on the day. The pound        was down about 0.21 percent\par to $1.8430.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders detected the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily last week, although there was no official confirmation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greenspan drew heavily on two speeches he gave last year in which he cautioned it was "imperative" for the United States and the global economy to thwart "creeping protectionism."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed chief said that if the globalization of trade is allowed to proceed unimpeded, then the likelihood is that current imbalances will resolve themselves with minimum pain.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Board Governor Mark Olson reiterated Greenspan's comments by saying first-quarter U.S. growth looked set to match the expected solid pace at the end of last year, but there was still no sign of inflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Low U.S. interest rates are continuing to punish the dollar against higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trichet's warning on Monday that "brutal" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves were unwelcome knocked the euro back from a record peak around $1.2898, braking a rally of around 13 percent since early November.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar dipped briefly after U.S. December import prices rose a modest 0.2 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now that the ECB has made its displeasure clear over the pace of the euro's rise, analysts said a greater sense of two-way risk had returned to the market and the much-talked about target of $1.30 per euro was no longer a given.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But analysts doubt the G7 would specifically warn against the dollar's fall as Washington seems relaxed about it, given low U.S. inflation and the presidential election in November. (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York and Natsuko Waki in London).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040114e01d0014z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
