\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro rally pauses as ECB warning prompts caution.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 10:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The euro held a cent below the previous day's record high against the dollar on Tuesday after a warning shot from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jean-Claude Trichet helped take the steam out of its rally.  \par \par \uc2 The market was now waiting to see what hints Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan would give about the U.S. economy and the outlook for interest rates at a Berlin event later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet's warning on Monday that brutal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were unwelcome knocked the euro back from a record peak around $1.2898, braking a rally of around 13 percent since early November.  \par \par \uc2 "Whether Trichet is complaining or not the euro is overextended and the euro/dollar rally is running out of steam," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London.  \par \par \uc2 "Who wants to go in at $1.28 or $1.27 and put on a big long euro/dollar position? Nobody is going to do that so we're going into profit-taking mode."  \par \par \uc2 By 0910 GMT the euro was holding around $1.2780, up a third of a percent from late New York levels.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was 0.25 percent lower on the day at 106.50 yen, confined to a narrow trading range by market nervousness that Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the greenback sliding lower against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was detected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to curb yen gains last week, although there was no official confirmation the authorities had taken action.  \par \par \uc2 GAUGING THE RISK  \par \par \uc2 Now the ECB has made its displeasure clear over the pace of the euro's rise, analysts said a greater sense of two-way risk had returned to the market and the much-talked about target of $1.30 was no longer a given.  \par \par \uc2 That said, few expected Trichet's comments to herald actual market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the ECB.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, the market was looking ahead to a Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers' meeting in Florida in February, to see whether policymakers would have a coherent position on the dollar's general decline.  \par \par \uc2 "Trichet's comments yesterday were stronger than those he has made recently and the size of the euro's pullback reflects increasing concern ahead of the G7 meeting," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a dangerous tactic and Trichet did not threaten imminent action, but his comments were enough to trigger some profit-taking."  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan is not expected to say anything about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he talks about globalisation in Berlin later.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers were on the lookout for comments relating to U.S. interest rates in light of a much smaller-than-expected rise in December employment figures released last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "Whatever Greenspan says probably won't have a positive effect on the dollar. But all the same, it shouldn't be too negative," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Fed officials Sandra Pianalto and Mark Olson are also due to speak later in the day.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  THREAT  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a record 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and traders suspect it has not changed tack this year.  \par \par \uc2 Reinforcing that view, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki warned recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves had been rapid given U.S. economic fundamentals and that Japan would take steps against speculative movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Caution also set in that the Bank of Japan may come under pressure to do more to increase money supply, in part to help weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on top of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most widely watched measure of money supply, M2 plus certificates of deposit (CDs), slowed in December, growing 1.5 percent from a month earlier, compared with 1.6 percent last November and 2.2 percent in December 2002.  \par \par \uc2 The sluggish growth, along with data showing a 5.1 percent drop in bank lending in December, showed that banks and borrowers were still focusing on cutting risk and debt.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040114e01d00143\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
