\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar firms after Greenspan speech.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 18:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The dollar rose more than a cent against the euro on Tuesday, retreating from Monday's record low after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan restated there is no sign of stress in funding the massive U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, meanwhile, was still digesting a warning shot from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet that helped take the steam out of its recent rally.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan said the dollar has fallen broadly against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Yet inflation, the typical symptom of a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , appears quiescent," the Fed chief said at Germany's Bundesbank in Berlin.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar gained 0.3 percent against the euro in initial reaction to Greenspan's remarks to trade around $1.2750 and remained steady by late morning in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "There is nothing (in Greenspan's speech) suggesting that anything is imminent in terms of policy coordination, including going into the G7 on Feb 7," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley & Co. in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "There is an awareness that the Europeans have put on the agenda the issue about what they believe (to be) too rapid a rise of the euro, so markets are now alert to the potential of policy response through direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or through a rate cut," Stewart said.  \par \par \uc2 Global policy makers of the Group of Seven industrialized nations are to meet in Florida on Feb. 6-7.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was down 0.43 percent on the day to 106.23 yen, confined to a narrow trading range by market nervousness that Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to curb yen gains.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.2240 francs, down about 0.33 percent on the day. The pound was down about 0.28 percent to $1.8417.  \par \par \uc2 Traders detected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily last week, although there was no official confirmation.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan drew heavily on two speeches he gave late last year in which he cautioned it was "imperative" for the U.S. and the global economy to thwart "creeping protectionism."  \par \par \uc2 The Fed chief said that if the globalization of trade is allowed to proceed unimpeded, then the likelihood is that current imbalances will resolve themselves with minimum pain.  \par \par \uc2 Low U.S. interest rates are continuing to punish the dollar against higher-yielding rivals.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet's warning on Monday that 'brutal' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were unwelcome knocked the euro back from a record peak around $1.2898, braking a rally of around 13 percent since early November.  \par \par \uc2 "Trichet used an international forum to express his concern. That injected some caution in the market and people are wondering how the G7 will react to the dollar's fall at its February meeting," said Marvin Barth, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Citibank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dipped briefly after U.S. December import prices rose a modest 0.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 TWO-WAY RISK RETURNS  \par \par \uc2 Now the ECB has made its displeasure clear over the pace of the euro's rise, analysts said a greater sense of two-way risk had returned to the market and the much-talked about target of $1.30 was no longer a given.  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of the G7 finance ministers and central bankers' meeting, European officials have expressed concern over the euro's strength.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, French Finance Minister Francis Mer said there would be an attempt at the meeting to send the 'correct signal' to markets on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that a firmer euro would be a disadvantage if it kept strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts doubt the G7 would specifically warn against the dollar's fall as Washington seems relaxed about it, given low U.S. inflation and a presidential election in November. (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York and Natsuko Waki in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040113e01d003q9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
