\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro hovers below peak ahead of Greenspan.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 15:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, changes dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The euro on Tuesday hovered more than a cent below Monday's record high against the dollar, still digesting a warning shot from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jean-Claude Trichet that helped take the steam out of its rally.  \par \par \uc2 The market was waiting to see what hints Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan would give about the U.S. economy and the outlook for interest rates at a Berlin event later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan is slated to speak on globalization in Berlin around 11:15 a.m. EST (1615 GMT), followed by a discussion.  \par \par \uc2 Investors are on the lookout for any comments Greenspan may make on U.S. interest rates in light of a surprisingly small rise in December U.S. employment. Fed officials Sandra Pianalto and Mark Olson are also due to speak later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 Although traders will listen carefully to Greenspan's remarks as their focus intensifies on global policy makers comments ahead of a Group of Seven industrialized nations meeting in Florida on Feb. 6-7, "people will be very surprised to hear any talk of a rate hike in the U.S. despite the good (recent U.S.) economic data," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital in Warren, New Jersey.  \par \par \uc2 Low U.S. interest rates continue to punish the dollar against higher-yielding rivals.  \par \par \uc2 Early morning in New York, the euro was up about 0.1 percent on the day to $1.2754. Against the yen, the dollar was down 0.4 percent on the day to 106.25 yen, confined to a narrow trading range by market nervousness that Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to curb yen gains.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.2237 francs, down about 0.3 percent on the day. The pound was up about 0.1 percent to $1.8488.  \par \par \uc2 Traders detected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily last week, although there was no official confirmation.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet's warning on Monday that 'brutal' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were unwelcome knocked the euro back from a record peak around $1.2898, braking a rally of around 13 percent since early November.  \par \par \uc2 "Trichet used an international forum to express his concern. That injected some caution in the market and people are wondering how the G7 will react to the dollar's fall at its February meeting," said Marvin Barth, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Citibank.  \par \par \uc2 "We have seen a pretty strong reaction from European accounts to go long dollars on the back of the ECB President's comments," said Leaver of Gain Capital.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dipped briefly after U.S. December import prices rose a modest 0.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The import price index does show that there is still very little inflation and pricing pressures even with a weaker dollar. It would suggest a continuation of low interest rates and that the dollar has room to weaken further," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 TWO-WAY RISK RETURNS  \par \par \uc2 Now the ECB has made its displeasure clear over the pace of the euro's rise, analysts said a greater sense of two-way risk had returned to the market and the much-talked about target of $1.30 was no longer a given.  \par \par \uc2 "Whether Trichet is complaining or not, the euro is overextended and the euro/dollar rally is running out of steam," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London.  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of the G7 finance ministers and central bankers' meeting officials in the euro zone have expressed concerns about euro strength.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, French Finance Minister Francis Mer said there would be an attempt at the meeting to send the 'correct signal' to markets on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that a firmer euro would be a disadvantage if it kept strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts doubt the G7 would specifically warn against the dollar's fall as Washington seems relaxed about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given low U.S. inflation and a presidential election in November. (Additional reporting by Natsuko Waki in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040113e01d0038c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
