\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro rally falters as ECB warning injects caution.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 12:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The euro held around a cent below the previous day's record high against the dollar on Tuesday after a warning shot from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jean-Claude Trichet helped take the steam out of its rally.  \par \par \uc2 The market was now waiting to see what hints Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan would give about the U.S. economy and the outlook for interest rates at a Berlin event later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet's warning on Monday that 'brutal' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were unwelcome knocked the euro back from a record peak around $1.2898, braking a rally of around 13 percent since early November.  \par \par \uc2 "Trichet used an international forum to express his concern. That injected some caution in the market and people are wondering how the G7 (Group of Seven) will react to the dollar's fall at its February meeting," said Marvin Barth, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Citibank.  \par \par \uc2 By 1045 GMT the euro was holding around $1.2743, steady on the day. The dollar stood at 106.50 yen, confined to a narrow trading range by market nervousness that Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to curb yen gains.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was detected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily last week, although there was no official confirmation the authorities had taken action.  \par \par \uc2 TWO-WAY RISK RETURNS  \par \par \uc2 Now the ECB has made its displeasure clear over the pace of the euro's rise, analysts said a greater sense of two-way risk had returned to the market and the much-talked about target of $1.30 was no longer a given.  \par \par \uc2 "Whether Trichet is complaining or not the euro is overextended and the euro/dollar rally is running out of steam," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London.  \par \par \uc2 "Who wants to go in at $1.28 or $1.27 and put on a big long euro/dollar position? Nobody is going to do that so we're going into profit-taking mode."  \par \par \uc2 Donald Johnston, head of the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) said the main concern was over the speed of the dollar's fall against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than the actual level. The OECD has repeatedly said however the dollar has to fall to correct global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 The market is now looking ahead to a Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers' meeting in Florida in February.  \par \par \uc2 Japan and the euro zone have already expressed concerns but analysts doubt the G7 would specifically warn against the dollar's fall as Washington seems relaxed about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given low U.S. inflation and the prospect of a presidential election later this year.  \par \par \uc2 "The consistent message from the U.S. is that for them as long as a dollar decline is orderly it is an unambiguously good thing as inflation is at a 40-year low and falling and depreciation of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases import substitutes (domestic goods)," Barth said.  \par \par \uc2 "It will be very difficult to get the U.S. to sign off a statement which tries to stem the dollar decline or, worse, conduct concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Investors are on the lookout for any comments from Greenspan relating to U.S. interest rates in light of a much smaller-than-expected rise in December employment figures released last Friday. Fed officials Sandra Pianalto and Mark Olson are also due to speak later in the day.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040113e01d002k1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
