\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK:  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Managers Switch To Yen From Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Adriana Arai  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 927 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 14:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The euro's strong start this year has propelled it to nearly $1.30 amid broad dollar weakness. Some analysts are even saying it's on its way to $1.40.  \par \par \uc2 But fund managers who specialize in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have a better bet to make money in 2004: the yen.  \par \par \uc2 It's what most of the world's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  money managers are switching into after profiting from a surging euro last year.  \par \par \uc2 Despite massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to stem its rise, they argue that the yen benefits from Asia's strengthening economic fundamentals and is therefore less vulnerable than the euro to a possible turnaround in dollar sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, the euro has risen more than 20% against the dollar in the past 12 months, almost twice as much as the yen - a fact which further supports a switch into yen. In European afternoon trade Tuesday, the euro was at mid-$1.27, little changed from Monday's close but off Monday morning's record high of $1.29. The dollar was around Y106.40, down from Y106.70.  \par \par \uc2 "Both the euro and the yen will be strong in 2004, but there's more value in the yen," said Andrew Dales, who manages some GBP24 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets at Barclays Global Investors in London. "There's a lower risk of yen weakness in the medium term, he added.  \par \par \uc2 "On a long-term valuation basis, the euro is now getting to a point where it's vulnerable to positive growth surprises in the U.S."  \par \par \uc2 Investors also see an emerging risk that European policymakers might take action to prevent the euro from rising further. This week, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jean-Claude Trichet said the ECB was "concerned" about what he described as "excess volatility and brutal moves" in the euro exchange rate. A strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could delay economic recovery in the 12 nations that use the euro, by making their exports more expensive.  \par \par \uc2 But that's also a concern for Japan, where the Bank of Japan has been aggressively manipulating markets to keep a lid on the yen. It spent a record Y20.057 trillion on buying mostly dollars last year, financing nearly half of the U.S. external deficit for 2003 in the process.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has continued strong this year, the BoJ buying as much as $40 billion for yen last week alone to keep the dollar above Y106, dealers estimate.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                  Fundamentals Favor Yen \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When comparing the likely effect of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  managers point out that the euro's surge is more of a speculative bubble - it's strengthening because investors don't want to buy the dollar and are looking for an alternative.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen, on the other hand, is rising because foreign and domestic investors are resuming investing in Japanese markets for the first time in several years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thus, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can at best slow the yen's upward trend, while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the euro could prove more effective.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs Asset Management analysts estimate the dollar would be in the Y85-90 region by year-end if there were no \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But with Japanese authorities continuing selling the yen, a move towards Y95-100 appears more likely, they said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, there are good reasons to believe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will reduce \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2004: increased U.S.-led political pressure against global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation in an election year, and expectations of an economic upturn in Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As far as politics goes, the Group of Seven richest nations are widely expected to warn against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation at their Feb. 6-7 meeting in Florida. A September G7 statement urging more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility across the globe sent the yen and the euro sharply higher, as investors interpreted it as the group's endorsement of a weaker dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If growth picks up in Japan in a cyclical recovery, maybe there will be less need to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Matthew Cobon, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  director at Deutsche Asset Management. "It will be difficult to see another year of massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Paul Duncombe, who oversees management of more than $40 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at State Street Global Advisors, says he thinks the Japanese will vigorously defend the Y100 level. He doesn't expect this year to see \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves of the magnitude of 2003 but says the euro may still be a good bet.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're more than halfway through (the dollar's depreciation process), but I don't think the euro will start looking expensive until the mid-1.30s," said Duncombe.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of course, both yen and euro bulls will need an even weaker dollar in 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A lot of the weakness has already happened, but we still see a better than 50% chance of the current trends being extrapolated in 2004," said Harriett Richmond, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at J.P. Morgan Fleming. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets tend to exaggerate trends."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And so far they're getting what they want. Not even the prospect of economic recovery in the U.S. is helping reverse the dollar's bleak outlook. Signs of strong growth failed to do the trick in 2003, so fund managers see no reason to believe they would this year either.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Their conclusion is that the U.S. must reduce external borrowing, which requires a weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to make the country's goods and assets look attractive again for foreign buyers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 207 842 9483 adriana.arai@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040113e01d0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
