\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Ends Mostly Lower; Market Ponders Greenspan Words   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Flint and Jamie McGeever   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 22:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar slipped against most major rivals Tuesday, despite an intraday rally after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan appeared to echo European official concern over the impact of a stronger euro.   \par \par \uc2 In a lecture on globalization in Berlin, Mr. Greenspan said a falling dollar puts European exports under "considerable pressure." Coupled with remarks by euro-zone officials, the Fed chief's comments spurred a short-lived rally for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against its European counterparts. The euro fell as low as $1.2715, sterling dipped to $1.8410, and the dollar spiked up to 1.2280 Swiss francs.   \par \par \uc2 However, analysts generally decided Mr. Greenspan had maintained a relaxed tone on dollar weakness and potential problems in funding the U.S. current account deficit, which left the dollar giving back some of its gains as the North American session progressed.   \par \par \uc2 "Greenspan's comments were very interesting. He was very, very sanguine about the correction of the trade deficit in the U.S., perhaps a little too sanguine," said Rebecca Patterson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan in New York.   \par \par \uc2 "I think policy makers are concerned about the pace of the dollar's moves, not necessarily the level," she added.   \par \par \uc2 In late trading, the euro was at $1.2751, up slightly from $1.2740 late Monday, but down against the yen at 135.48 yen from 135.95 yen. The dollar declined to 106.25 yen from 106.70 yen, and was also down at 1.2227 Swiss francs from 1.2280 francs late Monday in New York. Sterling ended at $1.8447, weaker than $1.8465 late Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Mr. Greenspan's comments on European exports come amid a rising tide of official rhetoric on the issue, highlighted Monday in remarks by his counterpart at the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Jean-Claude Trichet warned that the recent "brutal" moves and "excessive volatility" in exchange rates are unwelcome. On Tuesday, Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke went further and said euro-zone monetary officials are watching the euro's exchange rate closely.   \par \par \uc2 "We fear that the euro's appreciation ... could slow down economic growth" in the euro zone and also in Germany, its biggest economy, which relies strongly on exports, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Mr. Welteke, a member of the ECB's governing council, did add that the bank values a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but dealers appeared to pay more attention to the concerns about the impact the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has on exports and growth prospects. The euro hit a record high just shy of $1.29 on Monday, marking a jump of more than 7% against the dollar in barely over a month.   \par \par \uc2 In contrast to his European counterparts, Mr. Greenspan sounded "largely unperturbed" in the face of the dollar's decline, noted Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at MG Financial Group in New York.   \par \par \uc2 "All this suggests that verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to be largely the work of the ECB and euro-zone politicians, with the Fed highly unlikely to step in verbally or \b \highlight22\uc2 operationally\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he stated.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the return of Japanese investors and dealers to the market after Monday's public holiday in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought back into focus potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight the strengthening yen.   \par \par \uc2 According to the Kyodo News Agency, Japan's government sold about five trillion yen worth of U.S. Treasurys to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday to procure yen for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar. The report said it was the first time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has done so in 17 years and the volume is a record amount in the history of government sales to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The sale underscores the limits to which Japan's government is being pushed because of the massive amounts it is spending to support the dollar. However, Tuesday's transaction is most likely an accounting measure, which doesn't have direct impact on either \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  or bonds.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese monetary authorities have spent an estimated four trillion yen this year alone in trying to stop the yen from getting too strong. The Japanese government has a long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen appreciation, as a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen as damaging chances for an export-led economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 Other Asian monetary authorities are also trying to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a competitive rate that will help sales of exports abroad. The Bank of Japan's counterparts in Thailand and South Korea have been in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week actively selling their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a total of HK$1.59 billion Tuesday, buying about US$205 million, during local business hours as it continued its regular market activities aimed at stabilizing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 Since the Hong Kong dollar first strengthened beyond its peg rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar in late September, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold more than HK$50 billion in the markets, including Tuesday's transactions, to stymie the local unit's rise. The sales are now happening nearly every day, with the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling HK$4.17 billion Monday in similar transactions.   \par \par \uc2 After the last of Tuesday's sales, the U.S. dollar was quoted around HK$7.7630, still within its recent tight range. Traders said the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to be selling Hong Kong dollars to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from moving any higher than that level.   \par \par \uc2 -By Robert Flint, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4408; robert.flint@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 (Grainne McCarthy contributed to this report.)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040113e01d00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
