\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits life low vs euro then gains on Trichet.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2004\par 14:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 12 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a new record low against the euro on Monday but regained its footing after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Jean-Claude Trichet expressed concern about "brutal" \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 Investors, although persistently keen to sell dollars, are also listening closely to major policymakers for comments showing any international discontent with the rapid pace of the euro's rally.  \par \par \uc2 And many took Trichet's comments, made during a news briefing after a Group of 10 (G10) central brankers' meeting in Basel, Switzerland, as a sign of increased concern.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, Trichet also spoke about the euro but the interpretation of his statements was mixed with some analysts saying he showed little discontent.  \par \par \uc2 "His comments were definitely stronger than what he said last Thursday," Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One in London. "They are concerned about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and they are certainly not indifferent to what is going on."  \par \par \uc2 By 1330 GMT the dollar had strengthened on the day to stand at $1.2810 to the euro. Earlier it fell as low as $1.2898 per euro, its weakest since the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's launch five years ago.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier the dollar also slipped to $1.8577 against sterling, its weakest since September 1992.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar held steady against the yen throughout the day, just 0.1 percent down at 106.45 yen, with continued wariness that Japanese authorities could step in to sell yen to slow its export-damaging rise against the greenback. The dollar jumped two yen on Friday in a bout of suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 EURO RALLY TURNS HEADS  \par \par \uc2 Rampant dollar selling has been driven largely by worries over the current account deficit in the U.S. and expectations that U.S. interest rates will remain low for some time, making it difficult to attract foreign financing for the shortfall.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, this accelerated after a disappointing U.S. jobs report, which convinced markets the "considerable period" the Federal Reserve has promised to keep rates low could last even longer.  \par \par \uc2 Japan, which has seen the yen rise sharply as the result of dollar weakness, has already complained about its impact on exports. Now, concerned voices appear to be rising out of Europe more frequently as well.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking as the head of G10 central bankers, Trichet said Europe viewed volatile and brutal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements as unwelcome.  \par \par \uc2 "There was a mention by Europe that excess volatility and brutal moves were not welcome and not appropriate," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We are concerned. We are not indifferent." he added, referring to Europe.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking about risks to global recovery, he said: "There was a feeling that we have to remain vigilant. Confidence and vigilance are the two words that would sum up, for me personally, our meditation."  \par \par \uc2 However, Trichet also said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not predominate in a discussion that focused on building global economic recovery. This, some analysts said, mitigated the impact of his other comments on the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier on Monday, a Group of Seven (G7) source also told Reuters finance ministers from the world's richest nations would discuss tensions arising from developments in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at their summit in the United States early next month.  \par \par \uc2 But the source said that Washington seemed content with the weakening dollar, ruling out chances of the U.S. agreeing to issue a joint statement from the G7 about the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts believe little can be done to stem the dollar's fall if the U.S. remains quiet.  \par \par \uc2 Two manufacturing reports were also scheduled out of the U.S. in New York trading hours. The Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City releases its December manufacturing survey at 1600 GMT, while the Chicago Fed releases its manufacturing index for November at 1700 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Asian trading also had been subdued because of a holiday in Japan.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040112e01c003a2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
