\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar shifts gears, falls hard after jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 18:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - The dollar abruptly erased early gains against the euro on Friday, notching a fresh record low following a shockingly small gain in December U.S. non-farm payrolls.  \par \par \uc2 The Labor Department said non-farm payrolls added just 1,000 jobs in December. The puny gain was in sharp contrast to the 130,000 new jobs that had been expected. Furthermore, the November report, which initially showed creation of 57,000 jobs, was revised down to show a rise of 43,000.  \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate, however, fell to 5.7 percent from 5.9 percent in November.  \par \par \uc2 "We certainly expect that this is going to support the (Federal Reserve's) position that rates should remain on hold," said T.J. Marta, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Citibank in New York. "This certainly is going to cause a continued weight on the U.S. dollar. It's hard to believe this report."  \par \par \uc2 The news triggered a sharp sell-off in the dollar against most key rivals. The greenback had been broadly higher just ahead of the jobs report.  \par \par \uc2 In midmorning New York trade, the euro was up 0.67 percent percent at $1.2842, having scored a record high of $1.2868, according to Reuters data. The dollar was up 0.37 percent at 106.51 yen. The dollar fell to 7-year lows against the Swiss franc around 1.2195 francs and was last down 0.54 percent at 1.2201 francs. The euro was up 1.02 percent at 136.84 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "These data are being taken as evidence that the economic recovery could be slower than we have come to believe, and that would imply lower inflationary pressures and delayed Fed tightening," said Patrick Fearon, economist at AG Edwards & Sons in St. Louis.  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR HOLDS GAINS ON YEN  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, the dollar notched its highest level against the yen in almost a month after traders detected what may have been aggressive yen selling by Japan to slow the pace of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-choking rise. But the dollar had slipped back to near 106.80 by early New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan might be capable of postponing the dollar's fall but they can't single-handedly stop it," said Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Morgan Stanley.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not confirm any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but dealers said there was a continuation of yen selling seen earlier in the week.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen in 2003, a strategy that slowed the yen's rise against the dollar to around 10 percent for the year, half the euro's 20 percent advance.  \par \par \uc2 A market source on Wednesday told Reuters that Japanese authorities had spent three trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  early this week to keep the dollar from sinking below 106 yen.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPE TOLERANT  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone policymakers have so far appeared relaxed about the euro's record-busting rally.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet acknowledged on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was hurting euro zone exports, but added that growing global demand should mitigate the impact of a less competitive exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a feeling the ECB will only get concerned if volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets picks up and disorderly moves spread to other asset markets," said Bilal Hafeez, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 The market is focused on how policymakers will react to the dollar's fall at a meeting of the Group of Seven major industrialized countries in Florida on Feb. 6-7.  \par \par \uc2 Before that, central bankers gather in Basel on Monday for a meeting at the Bank for International Settlements. (Additional reporting by Christina Fincher in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e0190036j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
