\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls to record low vs euro on US jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 15:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a record low against the euro on Friday following a shockingly weaker than expected report showing U.S. nonfarm payrolls increased by only 1,000 jobs in December.  \par \par \uc2 The puny gain was in sharp contrast to the 130,000 new jobs that had been expected. Furthermore, the November report, which initially showed creation of 57,000 jobs, was revised down to show a rise of 43,000.  \par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate, however, fell to 5.7 percent from 5.9 percent in November.  \par \par \uc2 The news triggered a sharp sell-off in the dollar against most key rivals. The greenback had been broadly higher just ahead of the jobs report.  \par \par \uc2 "We certainly expect that this is going to support the (Federal Reserve's) position that rates should remain on hold," said T.J. Marta, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Citibank in New York. "This certainly is going to cause a continued weight on the U.S. dollar. It's hard to believe this report."  \par \par \uc2 In early New York trade, the euro was up 0.6 percent percent at $1.2832, having scored a record high of $1.2852, according to Reuters data. The dollar was up 0.32 percent to 106.47 yen. The dollar fell to 7-year lows against the Swiss franc around 1.2195 francs. The euro was up 0.94 percent at 136.75 yen.  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR HOLDS GAINS ON YEN  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, the dollar notched its highest level against the yen in almost a month after traders detected what may have been aggressive yen selling by Japan to slow the pace of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-choking rise.  \par \par \uc2 A thin market ahead of the U.S. jobs data provided ideal conditions for official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities, with the dollar rising 2 yen in a matter of minutes to push above 108.20 yen in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped back to near 106.80 by early New York trade and was a touch higher against the euro, off records lows reached earlier in the week.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan might be capable of postponing the dollar's fall but they can't single-handedly stop it," said Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Morgan Stanley.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not confirm any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but dealers said there was a continuation of yen selling seen earlier in the week.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said it was hard to pinpoint how much yen Japan had thrown at the market, but some suggested one trillion was a likely figure spent in the Tokyo session.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen on the foreign exchanges in 2003, a strategy that slowed the yen's rise against the dollar to around 10 percent for the year, half the euro's 20 percent advance.  \par \par \uc2 A market source on Wednesday told Reuters that Japanese authorities had spent three trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  early this week to keep the dollar from sinking below 106 yen.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPE TOLERANT  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone policymakers have so far appeared relaxed about the euro's record-busting rally.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet acknowledged on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise was hurting euro zone exports, but added that growing global demand should mitigate the impact of a less competitive exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a feeling the ECB will only get concerned if volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets picks up and disorderly moves spread to other asset markets," said Bilal Hafeez, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 The market is focused on how policymakers will react to the dollar's fall at a meeting of the Group of Seven major industrialized countries in Florida on Feb. 6-7.  \par \par \uc2 Before that, central bankers gather in Basel on Monday for a meeting at the Bank for International Settlements. (Additional reporting by Christina Fincher in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e019002t7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
