\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar gets Japanese tonic before U.S. jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 14:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 9 (Reuters) - The dollar leapt to a four-week high against the yen on Friday after what appeared to be aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the pace of the yen's export-damaging rise, but then gave up most of its gains.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said thin trading conditions ahead of a key U.S. jobs report provided ideal conditions for Japan's foray into the market, with the dollar jumping two yen in late Tokyo trade to rise above 108.20 yen for the first time since mid-December.  \par \par \uc2 But its bounce proved fleeting and the greenback had retreated to 106.80 yen by the European midsession. The dollar also struggled to retain any advantage against the euro, falling back within a cent of record lows hit earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 "We saw aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  which lifted the dollar to 108.23 yen, but if Japan had wanted to turn the trend around they would have stayed on the bid," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact they didn't shows this is still a strategy of just slowing the yen's rise."  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on whether it had ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but dealers said there was a continuation of yen-selling action seen earlier in the week.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said it was hard to pinpoint how much yen Japan had thrown at the market, but some suggested one trillion was spent in the Tokyo session.  \par \par \uc2 Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen on the foreign exchanges in 2003, a strategy that slowed the yen's rise against the dollar to around 10 percent for the year, half the euro's 20 percent advance.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPE TOLERANT  \par \par \uc2 The euro eased slightly to stand at $1.2738 at 1300 GMT, having stormed to historic highs above $1.28 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast to Japan which seems determined to curb the yen rise against the dollar, euro zone policymakers have so far appeared relaxed about the euro's record-busting rally.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet acknowledged on Thursday that the euro's rise against the dollar was hurting euro zone exports. But he added that growing global demand should mitigate the impact of a less competitive exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 "Trichet appeared to be relatively relaxed about the euro's rise," said Bilal Hafeez, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a feeling the ECB will only get concerned if volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets picks up and disorderly moves spread to other asset markets."  \par \par \uc2 The market is focused on how policymakers will react to the dollar's fall at a meeting of the Group of Seven major industrialised countries in Florida on February 6-7.  \par \par \uc2 Before that key central bankers gather in Basel on Monday for a meeting at the Bank for International Settlements.  \par \par \uc2 JOBS DATA  \par \par \uc2 U.S. jobs data at 1330 GMT could be key to the dollar's near term direction, with analysts predicting the Federal Reserve will be reluctant to raise interest rates until the U.S. labour market shows signs of significant improvement.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. interest rates at just one percent have dented the appeal of dollar deposits, encouraging capital to flow out of the United States at a time when the country needs to attract growing sums of capital from overseas to fund its current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters expect a 130,000 rise in December non-farm payrolls but do not expect these gains to translate into a fall in the unemployment rate which currently stands at 5.9 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is in a penalty box. If we have a good employment report it will be quickly dismissed. If we have a bad one the market will punish the dollar," said Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Morgan Stanley.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e019002k3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
