\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ may have sold Y1 trillion on Friday - traders.  \b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 10:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - Tokyo traders said Friday's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which briefly pushed the dollar up two yen, may have totalled about one trillion yen ($9.42 billion).  \par \par \uc2 While many traders said it was difficult to pinpoint an exact figure, some said the Bank of Japan (BOJ) had sold between 500 billion yen and one trillion yen to support the greenback. "It's hard to say if they put in one trillion (yen), but I wouldn't be surprised if it was over 500 billion," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 Within minutes in late Asian trading, the dollar leaped from its low on the day of 106.18 to 108.30 yen, only to fall back to the mid-106 yen level.  \par \par \uc2 "There's talk in the market that it was about one trillion yen, but it's hard to say," said a trader at a foreign bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Traders suspected that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's support was a continuation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week, when it was seen spending about three trillion yen ($28.25 billion) on Monday and Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "I guess they (the BOJ) have bought around $10 billion (on Friday)," said a dealer at a British bank.  \par \par \uc2 "I think they wanted to send a clear message that they want to protect the 105-yen level, which is not only a psychologically important level but is also a critical break-even point for Japanese exporters."  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was conducted through several banks.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) has declined to comment on the apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 While the market was confident that Japanese authorities would continue buying dollars to protect an export-led recovery in its economy, many players downplayed the impact of the actions.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts earlier in the week, the yen fell to a three-year low of 105.90 yen per dollar on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Whatever the amount was, it was a big amount, but in the end, it didn't do very much," one of the dealers said of Friday's suspected action.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As of 0901 GMT, the dollar was trading at 106.66  against the yen and\par $1.2717        against the euro.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , having been pulled up to 137.70 against the yen by the dollar's sudden vigour, had calmed down to 135.63 yen .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2003, the MOF spent a record 20 trillion yen in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . ($1=106.19 yen).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e019001sy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
