\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar soars vs yen on suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 08:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - The dollar shot up more than two yen on Friday to its highest level in almost a month after what appeared to be massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 However, the dollar's strength was fleeting, and it quickly lost half of its gains.  \par \par \uc2 Traders suspected that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's support was a continuation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week, when it was seen spending about three trillion yen ($28.25 billion) on Monday and Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "If that's the case, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (on Friday) must have been massive...it's hard to tell if they put in 1 trillion, but I wouldn't be surprised if it was over 500 billion," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 The jolt sent the greenback - recently weighed down by concerns over a bulging U.S. current account deficit - soaring to 108.30 yen, its highest level since December 15, from the day's low of 106.18.  \par \par \uc2 "I was looking at my screen and turned away to look at stock prices, and when I turned back, it had shot off the board," said Soichi Okuda, senior economist at Sumitomo Corporation.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) declined comment on whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 By 0712 GMT, the dollar had dropped back to 107.05 yen, suggesting that the effect of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to be short-lived. Still, that figure was almost a full yen above the day's low.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the BOJ initially stepped in to buy dollars at around 106.20 yen and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in heavy amounts after resistance at around 106.50 yen was cleared.  \par \par \uc2 Bankers said they then saw stop-loss buying by foreign operators and traders in Tokyo, which drove the dollar up to 108.30 yen, before exporters and Japanese life insurance companies stepped in to sell the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was pulled up against the yen by the dollar's sudden vigour, hitting 137.70 yen, a level not seen since July 29, 2003. It subsequently fell back to around 136.25 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was slightly weaker against the dollar, trading at $1.2730 versus $1.2756 in late New York.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said U.S. employment figures due at 1330 GMT and an upcoming Japanese public holiday on Monday compounded the effects of the BOJ support.  \par \par \uc2 "It started out with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but with the long weekend coming up, the market repositioned dollar shorts ahead of the U.S. payroll figures. So a lot of it had to do with timing," Okuda said.  \par \par \uc2 Market players were awaiting U.S. Labor Department figures for December non-farm payrolls.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of economists put the median estimate at 130,000 new jobs, compared with 57,000 new jobs in November.  \par \par \uc2 Interest in U.S. employment figures has been high, particularly ahead of this year's presidential election, as the data have been the eyesore of strong U.S. fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 However, many traders acknowledged that even if the figures were strong, they would not likely spark dollar-buying.  \par \par \uc2 HEAVY SPENDING  \par \par \uc2 Despite suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the start of the week, the dollar hit a three-year low of 105.90 yen on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 In 2003, the MOF spent a record 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Just before the dollar began its lunge higher on Friday, some traders had been considering the possibility that Japanese authorities would begin to ease on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities.  \par \par \uc2 "It's hard to grasp the intention of the authorities," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Itochu Corp following the dollar's dramatic rise and fall.  \par \par \uc2 "It was a very thrilling roller-coaster ride." ($1=106.19 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e019001hg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
