\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-BOJ steps up  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  but trend seen unchanged.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 10:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  signalled its strong commitment to halt a further rise in the yen by conducting massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday, initially pushing the dollar up more than two yen on the day.  \par \par \uc2 But an underlying dollar-selling trend fuelled by concerns over a widening U.S. current account deficit remained intact, and the dollar quickly lost about half the gains made from the yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ).  \par \par \uc2 "This is a clear message from MOF (the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to the market that they will stop the dollar from falling towards 105 yen," said a senior trader at a U.S. bank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had fallen to around 106.18 yen before jumping as high as 108.30 yen - its highest since December 15 - helped also by options-related buying.  \par \par \uc2 But by 0720 GMT it had slipped back to 106.66/71 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Market sentiment remains bearish for the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not change this," said Soichi Okuda, a senior economist at Sumitomo Corp.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the BOJ initially stepped in at around 106.20 yen and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in heavy amounts after resistance around 106.50 yen was cleared.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF declined to comment on the apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but a ministry official said the yen's recent strength was due to speculation and not based on economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told Reuters during a visit to Singapore that Japanese authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen overshoots but declined to comment when asked if they had taken action on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , conducted through several banks, could have totalled one trillion yen ($9.42 billion).  \par \par \uc2 A market source said Japan, worried that a strong yen could hurt an export-led recovery in its economy, spent about three trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday and Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 But the yen fell to a three-year low of 105.90 yen per dollar on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 RECORD \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 In 2003, MOF spent a record 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In a move to secure more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, the MOF announced last month that it would seek to raise the borrowing limit on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  account to 140 trillion yen in the fiscal year starting next April from 79 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 The increase of 61 trillion yen is equivalent to about $540 billion - roughly the annualised size of the U.S. current account deficit based on the latest third-quarter figures.  \par \par \uc2 As a stopgap measure to avoid a funding crunch before parliament approves the raising of the borrowing limit, the ministry has arranged a "repo" deal with the BOJ in which it can get funds by selling foreign bonds to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying the paper back at a later date.  \par \par \uc2 "They (the MOF) have plenty of funds now and so I think they are trying to show their strong posture that they will stand firm," said Sumitomo Corp's Okuda.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Toyota Motor Corp President Fujio Cho repeated his view that the yen was "a bit too strong" in light of Japan's economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 The Tokyo stock market's key Nikkei average closed at an 11-week high of 10,965.05 on Friday after the yen's fall triggered buying of shares of exporters and tech firms. ($1=106.19 Yen) (Additional reporting by Mariko Hayashibara).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e0190016i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
