\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar struggles as Trichet tolerant on euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 02:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 9 (Reuters) - The dollar struggled within sight of record lows against the euro on Friday, after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) chief said an improving global economy should help European exporters bear the burden of a strong euro.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet acknowledged on Thursday that the euro's recent record-busting rally against the dollar was hurting exports from the euro zone.  \par \par \uc2 But he added that growing global demand should mitigate the impact of the euro's rise, which makes European exports more expensive.  \par \par \uc2 With the guardian of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tolerant about the euro's rise, market players are expected to start dollar-selling again, given low U.S. interest rates and worries about the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "We still do not have any dollar-positive factors," said Kikuko Takeda, market economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0110 GMT, the euro was quoted around $1.2765 not far off its record high of $1.2813 marked on Tuesday and little changed from late U.S. levels.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback was stuck in a super-thin range around 106.20 yen amid the widely held belief that the Japanese authorities are determined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 106 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Late last year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  took several steps to ensure it had enough ammunition for dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and quell speculation that it was running out of such funds.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would seek to raise the borrowing limit for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 140 trillion yen ($1.318 trillion) in the fiscal year starting in April from the current 79 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 "Speculators are not so interested in buying the yen right now," said a dealer at a European bank.  \par \par \uc2 "There are many other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  they can buy and there's no point fighting with somebody who has tens of trillions of yen."  \par \par \uc2 With no sign of an end to the dollar's long decline, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Market players were awaiting the U.S. Labor Department report on December non-farm payrolls, due at 1330 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of economists put the median estimate at 130,000 new jobs, compared with 57,000 new jobs in November.  \par \par \uc2 "The data will be important as a sluggish labour market is one reason why the Fed wants to keep rates low for some time," said Takeda of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. ($1=106.19 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e0190006e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
