\par\par \b \uc2 OFF THE RUN: Fed Not Only  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Driving US Rates  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,073 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 20:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Agnes T. Crane \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Judging by the Treasury market's obsession with the Federal Reserve and its nuanced statements on the economy, it would seem there's room for only one \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the most influential fixed income market in the world. Not so.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 In fact, Asian central banks, most notably the Bank of Japan, have muscled in with their fist-full of dollars to represent a major source of strength for the bond market, in some respects playing a role equally as prominent as their U.S. counterpart.  \par \par \uc2 Stubbornly low yields in the Treasury market cannot be explained solely by the Fed's insistence that it will keep the federal funds target rate at 1.0% until inflation becomes a concern. Although such a commitment has encouraged hedge funds and other investors who take shorter-term views of the market to buy government securities in large quantities, the recent auction of $16 billion five-year notes was a reminder that foreign central banks are just as instrumental in keeping rates at such historical lows.  \par \par \uc2 Indirect bidders, which include foreign central banks, bought 40% of the issues sold, helping to push the award yield to 3.26%, one basis point lower than where it traded in the gray market where dealers trade new securities before they're actually sold ahead of the auction deadline. The strong interest, however, didn't come as a complete surprise to traders who noted the Bank of Japan had been gobbling up dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and reinvesting them in dollar-denominated assets in a bid to slow the weakening of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and insulate its fragile economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 That dollar buying has accelerated to a record pace. According to estimates circulating in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desks, the Bank of Japan, which acts on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has bought $40 billion in the first week of 2004, the same amount purchased in all of September 2003, a record month.  \par \par \uc2 "You can see that the pressure is intensifying and it takes much more money to stay the course they decided to stay on," said Sung Won Sohn, chief economist at Wells Fargo Bank. "Who knows how long the Bank of Japan can do this. Can they spend $40 billion every week? I don't think so."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par       Crank Up The Printing Press \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The momentum is certainly against them. A broad-based weakening of the dollar has pushed the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to all-time lows against the euro and to an 11-year trough against sterling.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Seemingly unfazed, the Japanese monetary authorities have asked the government for more ammunition to battle the strength of the yen - Y61 trillion, or three times the amount spent last year - to help its exporters by keeping the yen weak. While it's hard to quantify, bond market analysts agree that all of this money is having a profound impact on U.S. interest rates, and by extension on the deeply rate-sensitive, consumer spending-driven economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You definitely see there's a very strong relationship between the change in foreign custody holdings held by the Fed and rates," said Gerald Lucas, chief Treasury strategist at Banc of America Securities. Analysis by Banc of America found a 45% correlation between weekly fluctuations in 10-year yields and changes in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases of government securities held by the Federal Reserve.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of course, it's very difficult to quantify, but if he were to hazard a guess, Lucas reckons Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying alone has kept longer-dated Treasury yields at 25 basis points below where they would otherwise be.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  first stepped up its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back in May 2002, foreign central banks have added nearly $260 billion in U.S. Treasurys. Just to put that kind of spending in context, they sold about $20 billion in the two-year period beforehand.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Fed doesn't break down the data by individual bank, analysts note the number isn't far from the estimated $210 billion bought by the Bank of Japan during the same period in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lucas also reckons the Bank of Japan has more influence on longer-dated notes than say, the two-year note, even though it prefers to invest in shorter maturities "mainly because Fed expectations are the primary driver in the front end," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Then there's the People's Bank of China, which by way of strict capital controls and a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg, has accumulated billions of dollars that need a home. Like the Bank of Japan, it channels most of those dollars into U.S. Treasurys and other government debt securities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Japan, China Dominate \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan and Mainland China rank number one and number two, respectively, among the major foreign holders of U.S. Treasurys, according to the latest data. Japan held a mind-blowing $502 billion as of the end of Oct. 2003 while China, which recently usurped the U.K. for the number two spot, held $142 billion, according to data compiled by the U.S. Treasury.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Foreign central banks have been a big support to this market," said Michael Cheah, portfolio manager at SunAmerica Asset Management in Jersey City, NJ.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Not only did they help cushion the blow to markets when the government ramped up its debt issuance to finance its yawning budget deficit last year, but they promise to insulate the bond market from what would otherwise be negative shock waves from a falling dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PBOC, however, has more flexibility in terms of diversifying its holdings. Lucas said there are already signs that China is moving incrementally out of dollars and into the euro and gold, which puts the burden of supporting the dollar squarely on Japan's sagging shoulders.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But unlike China, "I doubt (the Bank of Japan) will deviate away from Treasurys significantly," but the pace of buying is unlikely to last, said Sohn of Wells Fargo.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even the Bank of Japan could run out of ammunition at some point," the economist said. "It's a matter of time. I don't see how they can \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the pace they are."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes T. Crane has covered credit markets for three years)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040109e019000pt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
