\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan 9  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,915 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities stormed into a sleepy Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Friday, pushing the dollar to a four-week high in its latest aggressive effort in a lonely campaign to stop the yen's rise. (WRAP will run by around 0905 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Finance Ministry will announce at the end of the month how much stopgap \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funding it procured in January by selling U.S. Treasurys to the Bank of Japan - a figure some analysts say could total Y5 trillion. ("=Japan MOF To Reveal At End Jan Tsys Amount Sold To BOJ," ran at 0810 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The government will unveil its new land sale program at a 0900 GMT press conference. Only a limited number of small and medium sites are likely be available for sale in 2004, as the government puts off major sales so as not to rock the still fragile homebuying sentiment. (Story will run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's trade surplus widened to $5.73 billion in December as exporters rushed to ship products to beat an end of year deadline before a cut in the country's export rebates. Exports rose by a faster-than-expected 50.7% year on year in December to $48.07 billion, outpacing an on-year rise in imports of 47.4% to $42.34 billion, Customs figures cited by a Ministry of Commerce statement issued Friday show. ("DATA SNAP: China Exports Surge Ahead Of Rebate Cut," ran at 0356 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's trade deficit stayed at its recent wide levels in November as a robust domestic economy encouraged imports and a strong Australian dollar crimped export values. The Australian Bureau of Statistics said Friday that the November trade deficit was A$1.73 billion, above the market consensus for a A$1.60 billion deficit and wider than October's revised A$1.70 billion shortfall. ("=DATA VIEW: Australia Trade Deficit Stays Wide On A$ Rise," ran at 0102 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: A flood of bonds will pour into Japan's credit market next week from major banks, government agencies and a few corporates, but analysts say strong demand means yields on the securities will stay tight to those for government bonds. ("=JAPAN CREDIT: Tight Spreads To Remain Despite Bond Flood," ran at 0658 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =BOJ WATCH: As the Bank of Japan moves to improve its communication with markets, it may have hit on a drawback. That's because some \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials may be worried that if the BOJ gives an honest assessment that the country's economy is stronger than it expected, bond investors could be spooked. ("=BOJ Watch: Upbeat View In Jan Report May Worry Bond Mkt," ran at 0333 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Limited inflation risks and strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across Asia should allow most central banks to keep monetary policy fairly loose this year. That's good news for the region's stock markets and for its economies, which have been relying on low interest rates to fuel domestic demand and help wean themselves from their dependence on export-led growth. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Asia To See Steady Rates In 2004," ran at 0221 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Though some analysts believe the forwards market shows overly optimistic expectations about the speed and scale of a possible revaluation of the yuan, technical analysis shows the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards at somewhat of a crossroads. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Charts Show 1-Year USD/CNY Discount May Narrow," ran at 0151 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China issued new rules on Friday aiming to increase the transparency of state-owned asset sales, and prevent abuse or losses that can occur in the process of such sales. ("China Issues Rules For More Transparent State Asset Sales," first take ran at 0049 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The International Monetary Fund said Friday it expects "important" news this week that China's government has injected $45 billion into two state banks to be followed up with other reform efforts that will strengthen the institutions' management. ("IMF/China," first take ran at 0852 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 COLOMBO: The Sri Lankan government hopes to attract a record 600,000 foreign tourists this year through increased room capacity and aggressive promotional campaigns, assuming peace on the island prevails, a senior government official said Friday. ("=INTERVIEW:Sri Lanka Hopes For 600,000 Tourists In'04," ran at 0854 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Nomura Securities Co. is upbeat about Japanese government bonds in 2004, primarily because it expects weaker U.S. economic growth will hurt Japan's own recovery. ("=INTERVIEW: Nomura Bullish On JGBs In 2004 As Econ Slows," ran at 0359 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's Asustek Computer Inc., the world's largest maker of computer motherboards, raised US$281.4 million in a five-year, zero-coupon overseas convertible bond issuance priced Thursday, a banker close to the deal said. ("Taiwan's Asustek Computer Raises US$281.4M From Conv Bond," ran at 0741 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong was again ranked the world's freest economy by the Heritage Foundation of the U.S., in the 2003 edition of its annual survey. The group said small, free economies like Hong Kong offer a good example to poorer countries seeking to develop their economies. (WRAP will run by around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Venture capital investment in mainland China more than doubled to US$992 million in 2003, according to a new survey, as investor interest in the country's vibrant high-tech sector staged a dramatic recovery after falling in 2002. ("China Venture Capital Invest Leaps To $992M In 03 -Survey," ran at 0204 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar surged to a one-month high against the yen late in Asia trade Friday on what market players described as aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Surges On Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ran at 0612 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Kookmin Bank, a main creditor of LG Card Co., said most of its demands on the card company's bailout plan had been accepted by the government, LG Card's creditors and LG Group. This removes a major obstacle in salvaging the credit card issuer, making it likely creditors will reach a final agreement on the bailout later Friday. ("Kookmin/LG Card," first take ran at 0700 GMT; further coverage as warranted)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Matsushita Electric Industrial Co. said Friday it aims to boost its profit margin to over 5% over the next three years partly through more cost cuts. (WRAP will run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGALORE, India: Infosys Technologies Ltd., one of India's biggest software services companies, Friday reported a stronger-than-expected 29% increase in third-quarter earnings as its clients boosted spending amid improved economic conditions in the U.S. The stock market bellwether, which is set to cross the $1 billion mark in annual revenue for the first time this fiscal year, said consolidated net profit rose to 3.28 billion rupees in the quarter ended Dec. 31 from INR2.55 billion a year ago. (WRAP will run by around 0905 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's United Microelectronics Corp., the world's second largest producer of custom-made semiconductors, said Friday revenue in December jumped more than 25% to NT$84.86 billion on the back of strong demand for chips used in mobile phones and other wireless applications. (Story will run by around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Creditors of Korea Thrunet may approve a restructuring plan for the high-speed Internet access provider as early as Friday, which would allow the company to continue its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  under court receivership. (Story may run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: A major initial public offering by Shinsei Bank Ltd. expected next month looks set to lure investors attracted to the strong earnings and relatively clean balance sheet of one of the troubled Japanese banking industry's success stories. ("=Japan Shinsei Bank IPO Looks Promising; Details Awaited," ran at 0833 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: UTStarcom Inc., a telecom network and equipment provider with extensive \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mainland China, has become a red-hot favorite among some analysts due to rapid growth in China's cut-price mobile phone services. ("=ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: UTStarcom Sparkles On China Ops," ran at 0606 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China Minsheng Banking Corp. said Friday its shareholders have approved plans to seek a Hong Kong share listing this year. ("China Minsheng Bk/HK IPO," first take ran at 0001 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's China Development Financial Holding Co. expects to earn NT$8.3 billion after taxes in 2004, turning around from a projected loss of NT$12.85 billion in 2003, according to a statement issued late Thursday. That would translate into a net profit of NT$0.8 per share in 2004, versus a loss of NT$1.4 per share last year. ("Taiwan's China Devt Forecasts '04 Net NT$8.3B Vs '03 Loss," first take ran at 0221 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: The fate of a $2 billion aluminum plant in east Malaysia's Sarawak state is uncertain after its backers failed to buy a controlling stake in a hydroelectric dam nearby, a source close to the deal said Friday. If the smelter's backers are to proceed with the project, guarantees are needed from the government, the industry source said on the condition of anonymity. ("=Malaysia Sarawak Aluminum Proj's Fate Uncertain - Source," ran at 0442 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Two debutantes on Hong Kong's Growth Enterprise Market soared higher on their first day of trade. SJTU Sunway Software Industry Ltd. opened 35% higher, while Tianjin Tianlian Public Utilities more than doubled its IPO price in intraday trade. (WRAP will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Fuji Television Network Inc. Friday announced a new equity financing plan to procure funds to build a new studio together with Nippon Broadcasting System Inc. ("Japan's Fuji TV," first take ran at 0659 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: With tourists slowly returning to conflict-torn Kashmir, India's state-run Jammu & Kashmir Bank Ltd. plans to focus lending on boosting the region's leisure industry. ("=INTERVIEW: India J&K Bk To Up Lending To Tourism Sector," ran at 0419 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: TPI Polene PCL, Thailand's third-largest cement producer, swung to a net profit in 2003 due mainly to huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains and extraordinary gains from investment. The company reported a net profit of THB4.78 billion for 2003, a turnaround from a loss of THB1.49 billion in 2002. ("Thai TPI Polene," first take ran at 0745 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: DBS Bank, a unit of DBS Group Holdings Ltd., may sell up to 40 million more covered warrants on rival Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp., a source close to the issue said Friday. This follows an earlier batch of warrants on OCBC and Thursday's issue on Singapore Airlines. ("DBS/More OCBC Warrants," first take ran at 0212 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Vietnam Airlines' revenues for 2003 were up 6.7% on year to 11.739 trillion dong, airline spokesman Nguyen Tan Chan told Dow Jones Newswires on Friday. ("Vietnam Airlines 2003 Revenues Up 6.7% At VND11.74T -Exec," ran at 0410 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040109e019000ax\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
