\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: U.S. Labor Data Grips Mkts  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 07:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Tech Shares Speed Higher \par  \par DJIA          10592.44   gain 63.41     up 0.6% \par NASDAQ        2100.25    gain 22.57     up 1.1% \par S&P 500       1131.91    gain 5.59      up 0.5% \par Dow Future    10572.00   gain 10.00     up 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1531.50    gain 0.50         0.0% \par S&P Future    1131.00    gain 1.50      up 0.1% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.25% unchanged \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--More signs of the improving business climate, including holiday sales above forecasts and Nokia Corp.'s latest outlook, sent stocks moderately higher Thursday. Treasurys held largely steady ahead of the U.S. labor report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: The advance carried the Nasdaq composite index to its first close above 2,100 in 2 1/2 years.  \par \par \uc2 The positive corporate news overshadowed a relatively poor reading on the job market  \par \par \uc2 "It seems that the good earnings news forced people into the market and overshadowed the jobless data," said Todd Clark, head of listed equity trading at Wells Fargo Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Nokia surged $2.50 to $20.47 in heavy trading, and shares in other makers of telecommunications equipment were also higher.  \par \par \uc2 That sector got another lift Thursday by an announcement from Verizon that it would invest $3 billion in networks over the next two years to bring broadband services to the mass market. Verizon was up 56 cents at $37.12.  \par \par \uc2 The Labor Department reported new claims for unemployment benefits increased last week following three straight weeks of declines, but the news wasn't bad enough to stifle optimism on the stock market.  \par \par \uc2 New applications filed for unemployment insurance rose to 353,000, an increase of 14,000 for the week ending Jan. 3, only slightly more than analysts had been expecting. That level of new applications in the previous week was the lowest since January 2001.  \par \par \uc2 Investors are looking ahead to Friday for a broader reading of the job market, when the government reports the overall civilian unemployment rate for December as well as figures on how many jobs were created or lost last month.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys were unchanged to modestly lower Thursday, ahead of the December employment report due Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Trading was generally subdued throughout the session as traders waited for what is regarded as the single most important piece of economic data for the bond market.  \par \par \uc2 Only a sloppy 10-year Treasury inflation-indexed note sale enlivened proceedings, sending the cash 10-year note near to its session low as dealers sought to hedge some of their unwanted TIPS holdings.  \par \par \uc2 However, by late afternoon, the market had trimmed its losses, with the payroll report taking center stage.  \par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires are forecasting job gains of 150,000 for December, with the unemployment rate staying unchanged at 5.9%. Some whisper estimates go well beyond 200,000. A number below 100,000 would prove bullish for Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 So far this week, the 10-year note yield has moved down from a high of 4.40% to a low of 4.23% earlier Thursday, as traders have trimmed bearish positions ahead of the labor report.  \par \par \uc2 A good payroll number will likely push the 10-year note yield back up to 4.35% to 4.40%, said Michael Krauss, chief technical strategist at JP Morgan in New York. A weak number is likely to spark a rally, but the 10-year yield will face a tough time getting below 4.10% to 4.14% area, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Japan Stocks Higher On Tech Gains \par  \par USD-Yen             107.56       gain  1.40    up 1.3% \par AUD-USD             0.7720       loss  0.0028  dn 0.4% \par Nikkei 225          11006.91     gain  169.26  up 1.6% \par Hang Seng           13333.97     gain  130.38  up 1.0% \par JGB Yield           1.3700% down 0.0050 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese stocks rose Friday morning following Wall Street's gains on signs of an improving U.S. business climate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors picked up technology and exporter blue chips after encouraging results for U.S. stocks on Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technology issues such as Advantest and Canon, and automakers Honda and Nissan gained Friday morning.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government said Friday its key gauge of the current state of Japan's economy stayed below the boom-or-bust line of 50% in November.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late in the Japanese session, the Bank of Japan was suspected of aggressively selling yen, prompting a jump in the dollar, according to traders.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Economy, Trade and Industry Minister Shoichi Nakagawa said Thursday that he has told a top White House economic adviser that the U.S. should take measures to prevent the dollar's decline, Kyodo News reported.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's total trade volume reached a record $851 billion in 2003, helped by export rebates and the decline in the yuan in tandem with its peg to the U.S. dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets Brace For Surprise In US Jobs \par  \par Euro-USD            1.2720      loss  0.0040  dn 0.3% \par Stlg-USD            1.8292      loss  0.0040  dn 0.3% \par USD-Franc           1.2305      gain  0.0035  up 0.3% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares are poised to open slightly higher, with government bonds and the euro at fairly steady levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares are likely to start higher ahead of the U.S. jobs report, backed by gains in the U.S. and Japan overnight. Spreadbettor IG Index is calling for marginal rises in the main European indices at the open.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks were higher Thursday, lifted by strong gains in telecommunications and technology stocks amid hopes that the sector may be on the road to recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, analysts said equity gains may be tempered as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  concerns continue to simmer.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The euro has risen by over 40% versus the dollar over the past two years," economists at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein said. "These are big moves and will impact profits." They estimate that for every 10% rise in the euro, there's a 5% hit to profits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are likely to open little changed, in line with Treasurys and Japanese government paper, ahead of the crucial U.S. labor report at 1330 GMT.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone government bonds gained Thursday, after the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left interest rates unchanged but, more significantly, made little attempt to curb recent euro gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategists at Lehman Brothers suggest the prospect of further euro gains will force the ECB to ease monetary policy in March.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro lost some ground overnight after suspected Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to aid the dollar. That action followed a dollar selloff on Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Thursday dollar selloff erased early gains after comments from euro-zone officials indicating a lack of resolve to counter the strength of the euro.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left their interest rates unchanged as expected. But comments by the new head of the ECB drove investors back into long euro positions that had been lightened following Tuesday's all-time high above $1.28.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet acknowledged the likely impact of the strong euro on exports, he generally downplayed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's risk to the euzo-zone economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "On the external side, recent exchange rate developments are having a negative impact on the price competitiveness of euro area exporters, but thus far this should be partly compensated for by the ongoing expansion of global demand," said Trichet. He added that exports should continue to grow overall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City President Thomas Hoenig said Thursday he expects robust and sustainable growth for the new year, of the sort that will likely improve employment levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I suspect we will look back on 2004 as the year economic activity became more self-sustaining," Hoenig said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Friday, Jan 9: German, UK Trade; US Jobs \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0200/0700 Germany   Nov Current Account  (last +EUR6.3 Bln) \par 0200/0700 Germany   Nov Trade Balance (last +EUR10.8 Bln) \par 0430/0930 U.K.      Nov Trade Balance (seen -GBP4.2B) \par 0830/1330 U.S.      Dec Non-Farm Payrolls (seen +125K) \par 0830/1330 U.S.      Dec Unemployment Rate (seen 5.9%) \par 0940/1440 U.S.      Dec ECRI Inflation Gauge (last 115.7) \par 1130/1630 Italy     Treasury details amount BTPs, CCTs \par 2000/0100 Canada    Bank of Canada Deputy Governor \par    N/A    Germany   Nov Manufacturing Orders (seen -0.5%) \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181; dennis.baker@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BP (BP.LN): Trading Update \par 