\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: US Payrolls To Pressure Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,079 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 19:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market intent on selling dollars until G7 and the Bush administration respond, sustaining meaningful dollar bounces requires high-octane U.S. economic news. Uber data. A 1,000 gain in non-farm payrolls is pathetic. And especially so given an 8% growth rate in Q3 and probably a 5% pace in Q4. And also especially given high corporate profits and high productivity rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Regarding the labor market, there is only one conclusion to draw: firms won't be adding workers anytime soon. They do not believe in the recovery. They do not want to add cost when demand remains well for lack of a better term fragile. I even think the notion that business spending is set to roar is misleading. Uncertainty quotient remains higher for firms than households and the level is nothing to diminish. No wonder farming out labor-intensive work to India and China is so appealing. So if firms coffers fill with profits in the medium-term, fine. What firm does not want to build cash balances, or recapitalize its pension fund?  \par \par \uc2 Do you hear the beat of deflation off in the distance?  \par \par \uc2 But more assuredly the softness in the labor market should convince most, if the November election has not, that the Fed is doing zippo on interest rates for much of this year. That is not good for the dollar. It means higher returns elsewhere will draw capital, all else being equal, away from the U.S. And the political economy of the soft labor market is perhaps most interesting. Even an "unelectable" Democratic presidential candidate starts to look "electable" with a jobless recovery, no matter what stock indexes are doing or GDP is printing. While I would not take a Dean win with a betting shop, even a close race will force the Bush administration to respond ... to labor-market weakness. It must take a stand against the exporting of jobs and this will make protectionist polices more appealing. It will also make a much lower dollar even more appealing to the White House. And then there are the fiscal policy implications. More tax cuts, making existing tax cuts permanent and extending unemployment benefits all come to mind (first two are already being proposed). One thing even ardent Democrats must concede is that the Bush Republicans learned a lot from Bill Clinton. When in need of broad public support steal policies from the opposition. Immigration reform, child tax credit, and leave-no-child-behind policies come to mind. So it is not unrealistic to expect some unemployment benefit extension or jobs program this year.  \par \par \uc2 Leaving euro/dollar and dollar/yen levels up to the markets, will be the hallmark of the Bush "strong" dollar policy. And with politics and economics telegraphing to the free competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell dollars, it is hard to see the conditions that would generate U.S.-backed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (as in using U.S. reserves under the direction and orders of U.S. Treasury). At the very least G7 is at least two meetings away from confronting the weak dollar ... G7 meets Feb. 6-7 in Boca Raton, Fla., and meets again in May in Washington ... and even then it is likely to be limited to a communique. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  involving the U.S. in some multilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a remote risk in 2004 shy of an assault on U.S. stock and or bond markets driven by a collapsing dollar.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           BoJ Cedes Monetary Policy To MoF \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In keeping with my argument Thursday that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unsustainable, I left out a key element of why the policy is so distorted ... it robs the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of control over monetary policy. By allowing MoF to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in ever larger amounts and ever more frequently to prevent dollar/yen from weakening, MoF has gained control over the major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mechanism for administering monetary policy ... amount of reserves (yen) in the system. One can only guess that the BoJ is not buying much paper from the banks to inject liquidity into the money market. Indeed a money market shortage requiring BoJ bill \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to meet should become increasingly rare ... as long as MoF continues dumping yen into the money market as it buys dollars. Some estimates have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week alone at $50 billion. One wire service reported Wednesday that a source said MoF/BoJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday and Tuesday totaled $28 billion. At the very least another $10 billion to $15 billion has been done the last three days. At this pace can the BoJ really say it has control over the money supply? Indeed the only control it has is to say no to opening swap lines (with MoF using its U.S. Treasury portfolio as collateral) to allow the government to sell ever more yen now that it is running into legal limits on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  funding (financing bills). Fukui is no Hayami. Former BoJ Governor Hayami was known in the halls of MoF as uncooperative. And in the MoF international bureau as anti-weak-yen ... i.e. anti-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and pro-yen. Can you imagine a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor in Japan who believes in a strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ? Not when you think about the ever pliable Fukui.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Again I think the next big \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trade is dollar/yen collapsing ... catching up with euro/dollar (watch out euro-yen longs). The problem is it is quite difficult to determine when the game is up for MoF and yen selling. At some point Japan will abandon this strategy, and that point is surely getting closer. But with Japan taking up huge amounts of U.S. government debt, do not look for U.S. Treasury Secretary Snow to call an end to the game. Indeed the very fact that he calls for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be set in free competitive markets and does not single out Japan as a serial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator is incredulous. The natural question is why has Europe been so passive over this U.S.-Japan arrangement?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By David Gilmore, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics trades in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040109e01900024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
