\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to sell Treasuries for  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  - Nikkei.   \b0\par\par\uc2 405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2004\par 19:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to sell several trillion yen worth of U.S. Treasuries as early as next week to make cash available for its massive dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nikkei's Nihon Keizai Shimbun Friday edition reported.   \par \par \uc2 The reported sale of Treasuries appears to be a stop-gap measure for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account until new legislation, due in late January, provides them with more cash for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei report said the government would buy back the bonds later in such a manner that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not suffer any losses from the arrangement.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in recent months in an effort to stop the yen from rising against the dollar with the hope of protecting its fragile export sector.   \par \par \uc2 On Dec. 26, the MOF, in an effort to quell speculation it would run out of cash to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said it could now raise funds by selling up to 10 trillion yen (about $94 billion) worth of foreign bonds - namely U.S. Treasuries - to the Bank of Japan in the form of repurchase agreements, Reuters reported.   \par \par \uc2 But at the time of that announcement, a MOF official said this deal with the BOJ was just a precautionary measure as the authorities still had ample funds.   \par \par \uc2 Now, according to the Nikkei report, it appears the MOF does not feel comfortable with the cash levels in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account.   \par \par \uc2 The reported move comes just a few weeks after the MOF's announcement that it would raise the legal ceiling for its issuance of short-term bills for its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account.   \par \par \uc2 The increase in the MOF's borrowing limit, spelled out in the draft budget for fiscal 2004/2005, would not be formally legislated until late January, and this decision to sell Treasuries to the BOJ is seen as a stop-gap measure.   \par \par \uc2 A market source told Reuters on Wednesday that Japanese authorities spent three trillion yen in the first two days of this week alone in order to keep the greenback from sliding below the 106 yen level. The dollar fell briefly below the 106 yen level on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Nikkei reported that after this yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the the government has spent all but 1 or 2 trillion yen of the 79 trillion yen in funds budgeted for foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fiscal 2003.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040108e0180039h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
