\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar drops - ECB's Trichet muffles euro concerns.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2004\par 18:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The dollar shed more than 1 percent against the euro on Thursday, reversing early gains after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said rising global demand should help European exporters bear the burden of a soaring euro.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB chief acknowledged that the euro's recent record-breaking rally against the dollar was hurting exports from the euro zone. But he added that growing global demand should mitigate the impact of the rise in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which makes European exports more expensive.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet, speaking at a news conference after the ECB held its key interest rate unchanged at 2 percent, said the price of the euro was one reason the bank decided to leave rates at historic lows.  \par \par \uc2 "In his post-meeting news conference, I think he has essentially maintained the status quo," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Ruesch International in Washington, D.C.  \par \par \uc2 "There was some risk that he might be critical of the recent speed and breadth of recent euro gains," Beuzelin said. "I don't think the comments were a reflection of any alarm or concern."  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the Bank of England left its key interest rate steady at 3.75 percent. Both the BOE and ECB rate announcements were expected and caused little splash in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar earlier made small gains against the euro as markets waited for Trichet to speak. Some speculated he might express greater concern over the euro's record-breaking rally.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has risen more than 10 percent against the dollar in the past two months, bringing its gains over the past 2 years to more than 40 percent. European business groups have warned that the euro's strength is denting growth prospects.  \par \par \uc2 Midday in New York, the euro was up 1.07 percent at $1.2774 , but off Tuesday's record high of $1.2812. The dollar was down 0.08 percent at 106.13 yen , hemmed into an extremely tight trading range as dealers remained wary of continued dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A market source told Reuters on Wednesday that Japanese authorities had poured three trillion yen into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the first two days of this week alone in order to defend the 106 yen level. This compares with 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through all of 2003, a level which was itself a record.  \par \par \uc2 Traders doubted how long Japan could hold back dollar selling against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 In other trading, the dollar was down 1.18 percent at 1.2250 Swiss francs . The Australian dollar rose more than 1 percent to US$0.7752 . Sterling rose 0.70 percent to $1.8305 .  \par \par \uc2 Earlier on Thursday, European Union Trade Commissioner Pascal Lamy said the euro's exchange rate against the dollar was approaching a level that could be worrying for competitiveness in the 12-nation single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar showed little reaction to earlier news that the number of first-time U.S. jobless claims rose to 353,000 in the week to Jan. 3 from 339,000 a week earlier. Economists had forecast a rise to 350,000. The four-week average declined to 350,000 claims from 355,750 the week before.  \par \par \uc2 "The shock value of the claims headline - up more than expected - may have helped the euro," said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York. "But the underlying data in the report are quite positive."  \par \par \uc2 All eyes are now fixed on Friday's U.S. Labor Department report on December non-farm payrolls. A Reuters poll of economists put the median estimate at 130,000 new jobs, compared with 57,000 new jobs in November.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040108e0180037a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
