\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steady vs euro, helped by ECB jitters.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2004\par 14:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The dollar pared its gains against the euro but was still two cents above recent record lows on worries the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might signal concern over the euro's record-breaking rally later on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB's kept interest rates at a historic low of two percent as expected but markets were focused on whether bank chief Jean-Claude Trichet would use his news conference at 1330 GMT to signal a shift in his hitherto relaxed attitude to the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement and European Union Trade Commissioner Pascal Lamy both expressed concerns about strength of the euro, which has leapt more than 10 percent against the dollar in the past two months, bringing its gains over the past two years to more than 40 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "There was a bit of nervousness about Trichet at first, but as the hour approaches maybe some people are taking their chips off the table of this dollar correction," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think Trichet will deliver any earth-shaking news."  \par \par \uc2 At 1305 GMT, the euro was steady on the day at $1.2624 after easing to $1.2562 in early European trade, 2-1/2 cents below record peaks scaled on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady near three-year lows around 106.00 yen, with dealers wary of continued dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Germany's Clement said he hoped for a signal on the euro from the ECB and that he favoured a reduction in interest rates given the state of the euro zone economy.  \par \par \uc2 Lamy said the euro's exchange rate against the dollar was approaching a level that could be worrying for competitiveness in the 12-nation single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone.  \par \par \uc2 However, traders say it may be difficult for the ECB to warn strongly against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength since bank officials have recently said the euro's value corresponded to long-term averages and that they wanted a strong and stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "If there is going to be a comment from the ECB it is more likely to focus on the speed of adjustment, as opposed to the level itself," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro.  \par \par \uc2 In terms of data, U.S. initial jobless claims for the week ended January 3 are due at 1330 GMT and expected to total 350,000 compared with 339,000 in the prior week.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier on Thursday, the Bank of England left interest rates steady at 3.75 percent for the second month in a row after having raised them for the first time in nearly four years in November.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WARINESS  \par \par \uc2 Senior Japanese Finance Ministry officials continued to lash out against the yen's rise on Thursday, warning that authorities would take decisive action against volatile moves.  \par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi said recent moves have been "very rapid" when asked about the yen's recent rise.  \par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the ministry's international bureau, warned that sudden moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were "damaging".  \par \par \uc2 Investors suspect the Bank of Japan has stepped up its forays in the market this week to slow the yen's potentially export-damaging appreciation against the sliding greenback.  \par \par \uc2 A source told Reuters on Wednesday that Japanese authorities poured three trillion yen onto the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the first two days of this week alone.  \par \par \uc2 This compares with 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the whole of 2003, a level which was itself a record.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had dipped below 106.00 yen on Wednesday before resurfacing almost instantly in a move attributed to a glitch on the EBS trading system, and banks in Tokyo used 105.90 as the overnight three-year low.  \par \par \uc2 Traders suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around 106 yen in U.S. trade on Wednesday to prop up the dollar. But many doubted for how long Japan would be able to deflect upward pressure on the yen from the dollar's broad-based decline.  \par \par \uc2 "Trading around 106 does appear to be quite a key level now for the Japanese monetary authorities," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 "Everything points to the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain an issue for the market."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040108e018002bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
