\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar wins respite as euro retreats before ECB.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2004\par 10:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The dollar won some respite against the euro on Thursday as nervousness the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might signal concern over the euro's record-breaking rally prompted investors to reduce bets on the greenback's slide.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB is expected to leave interest rates unchanged at Thursday's policy meeting and the market focus is on whether bank chief Jean-Claude Trichet will use a news conference at 1330 GMT to signal a shift in his hitherto relaxed attitude to the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has leapt more than 10 percent against the dollar in the past two months, bringing its gains over the past two years to more than 40 percent. Business groups have warned that euro strength is hurting European exporters and denting growth prospects.  \par \par \uc2 "The pace of the euro's advance is such that Trichet will have to say something, but it will be hard for him to deviate too far from his traditional line," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \par \par \uc2 "So far, all the bank has said is that a strong and stable euro is in the region's interest."  \par \par \uc2 The euro eased to $1.2564 in early European trade, down 2-1/2 cents from record peaks scaled on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was steady near three-year lows around 106.00 yen, with dealers wary of continued dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WARINESS  \par \par \uc2 Investors suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped up its forays in the market this week to slow the yen's potentially export-damaging appreciation against the sliding greenback.  \par \par \uc2 A source told Reuters on Wednesday that Japanese authorities poured three trillion yen onto the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the first two days of this week alone.  \par \par \uc2 This compares with 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the whole of 2003, a level which was itself a record.  \par \par \uc2 "That's an amazing amount," said a dealer at a British bank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar stood at 106.15 yen at 0920 GMT, little changed on the day. It had dipped below 106.00 yen on Wednesday before resurfacing almost instantly in a move attributed to a glitch on the EBS trading system, and banks in Tokyo used 105.90 as the overnight three-year low.  \par \par \uc2 Traders suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around 106 yen in U.S. trade on Wednesday to prop up the dollar. But many doubted for how long Japan would be able to deflect upward pressure on the yen from the dollar's broad-based decline.  \par \par \uc2 "They are just conducting a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yoshiharu Yanagisawa, vice president at State Street Bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "It's difficult to contain the yen's rise when global capital is moving away from the U.S. to the rest of the world."  \par \par \uc2 Persistent concerns over the health of the U.S. balance of payments have taken a heavy toll on the dollar, which fell around 10 percent against the yen last year and almost twice that against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. current account deficit is already running at around five percent of gross domestic product and analysts say a strong recovery in the United States may only exacerbate this deficit. The country 