\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar steady versus yen, market cautious of BOJ.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2004\par 02:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 8 (Reuters) - The dollar held steady against the yen on Thursday after dipping to a three-year low the previous day, supported by wariness over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the greenback suddenly fell below the closely watched 106 yen level to hit the low of 105.90 yen before resurfacing almost instantly.  \par \par \uc2 There was confusion over why the blip occured, with some dealers saying it was due to a glitch on the EBS trading system, but banks in Tokyo - both foreign and domestic - used 105.90 as the overnight low.  \par \par \uc2 Traders suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 106 yen during U.S. trade to prop up the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Still, few players expect a rebound in the dollar as players worry about the yawning U.S. current account deficit and with benchmark U.S. interest rates likely to stay at a 45-year-low in the near-term.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the market will test a break under 100 yen by the end of March," said Yoshiyasu Naruse, head of corporate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at HSBC Ltd.  \par \par \uc2 He said that Japanese exporters were lowering their yen-orders to around 107 yen per dollar and that level would likely temper any rebound in the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 A median forecast of 49 analysts polled by Reuters saw the dollar holding steady at 106 yen in the coming month but slipping to 105 in three months, 104 in six and 102 by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0108 GMT, the dollar was at 106.20/25 yen, hardly changed from late U.S. trade on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro slipped to $1.2599/03 from 1.2631/37 in late U.S. trade. It hit a record high of 1.2813 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 133.86/91 yen from 134.04/16.  \par \par \uc2 Traders were awaiting comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, who will hold a news conference at 1330 GMT after the bank's monetary policy meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The focus is on whether Trichet will sound off any warnings on the recent jump in the euro, although few analysts expect him to do so.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think he will change his tone," said Naruse.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) said the growing U.S. deficits raised the risk of an abrupt drop in the value of the dollar, which could hit U.S. and global economic growth. The IMF said it was a risk that "cannot be ignored".  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities, seen desperate to keep the dollar away from the technically and psychologically important 105 yen level, spent around three trillion yen ($28.25 billion) in the first two days of this week on yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a market source told Reuters on Wednesday.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a total 20 trillion yen throughout 2003 in trying to keep the yen in check. ($1=106.21 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040108e01800118\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
