\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Dec Foreign Reserves $673.53B; Up $28.96B On Month  \b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 00:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose $28.96 billion on month in December to $673.53 billion, Japan's Finance Ministry said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The data marked the fourth straight month of increases and another record high, highlighting the massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities in December to prevent a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from hurting the nation's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's reserves of convertible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gold and International Monetary Fund special drawing rights in December were up from $644.57 billion in November, the ministry said.  \par \par \uc2 The rise follows November's gain of $18.3 billion from previous month.  \par \par \uc2 The largest factor driving the latest increase was \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a Finance Ministry official briefing reporters said.  \par \par \uc2 According to data last month, the MOF ordered the Bank of Japan to sell Y2.252 trillion worth of yen in December. The total for 2003 was a record Y20.057 trillion, far bigger than the last record amount of Y7.64 trillion in 1999.  \par \par \uc2 In fact, because of the heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action during the year, the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  coffers are running low.  \par \par \uc2 Therefore, the MOF is set to raise its Y79 trillion funding limit for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Y100 trillion this fiscal year ending March, and then to Y140 trillion next fiscal year. The ministry is waiting for parliament to approve the increases sometime early this year.  \par \par \uc2 To take care of any emergency funding needs which may arise in the meantime, the government has also asked the BOJ to lend up to Y10 trillion in funds by buying U.S. Treasury bonds from the ministry under repurchase agreements. The Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported Friday morning that the MOF has decided to sell several trillion yen worth of U.S. Treasuries to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as early as the start of next week.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo is already suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in the New Year to the tune of around Y3 trillion, although the yen hit a 40-month high earlier this week of Y105.91 against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Bolstering the December reserves figure was the drop during the month in yields on 10-year U.S. Treasurys and European government bonds, as well as the euro's roughly 5% gain versus the dollar, the MOF official noted.  \par \par \uc2 Japan typically invests much of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it acquires via market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in U.S. Treasurys and European government bonds.  \par \par \uc2 According to ministry data, the dollar's high for the month was Y110.04 hit on Dec. 1, while the low was Y106.93 on Dec. 29. The euro's high was $1.2506 on Dec. 30, while its low was $1.1956 on Dec. 2.  \par \par \uc2 Last month's foreign reserves were up $203.8 billion from $469.73 billion in December 2002.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's foreign reserves were the largest in the world for the 49th consecutive month as of October, the latest month for which comparable data from most other nations are available, the ministry said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan had $617.9 billion in reserves, followed in second place by China with $389.8 billion, although the figures for China are for September. The euro zone was third with $252.5 billion, Taiwan was fourth with $197.2 billion, while South Korea came fifth with $143.2 billion.  \par \par \uc2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale and Hugh Lawson  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040108e018000sp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
