\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:Glitches, Credit Lines Add To Dollar's Misery  \b0\par\par\uc2 934 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2004\par 23:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Talk about kicking the dollar while it's down.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Already creaking under the host of economic and political reasons not to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar is getting no help from a growing number of reports of glitches in electronic trading systems and talk that some banks' credit lines with Japanese financial institutions are reaching their limit, raising the possibility of some intermittent problems in executing trades.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers have expressed mounting frustration this week with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  electronic trading system EBS, which they say is experiencing more and more technical problems. At the same time, the massive amount of dollars being bought by Japanese commercial banks on behalf of Japan's government is prompting talk that some of their counterparties are unable to execute orders because they've reached their credit limits with those Japanese banks, dealers add.  \par \par \uc2 These developments can have clear implications: In a hugely liquid market that's positioned one way - in this case, to sell dollars - any volatility or perceived disruption to normal trading, no matter how fleeting, can simply fuel the heavy selling pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the EBS price feed via Reuters machines on the trading floors of some banks in New York briefly froze, causing some market participants to lose money. "Some cross-(\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates) traders who look at their spread sheets which use these price feeds got hurt because they were behind the curve," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 With most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  surging rapidly at the time against the dollar - as European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet held a press conference in Frankfurt - dealers who had been betting on the dollar gaining were forced to scramble to cover these short positions, which added to the upward momentum.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers at a large European bank said their EBS price feeds in U.S. trading rooms went down briefly early in the day, with spreads on the euro/dollar exchange rate jumping as wide as 20 basis points compared to five or less normally. One U.S.-based dealer at the bank said he was still able to trade, however.  \par \par \uc2 An EBS spokesperson said Thursday there were "tiny glitches with the distribution feed to the ticker that goes through Reuters' systems for a minute or two," but that the EBS spot system was functioning normally.  \par \par \uc2 The disruptions came a day after EBS said it was investigating "isolated" complaints from clients regarding glitches with its system, mainly at Japanese banks. But anecdotal evidence gathered this week from London- and New York-based dealers at Japanese, German and Swiss banks suggests the technical problems are far from isolated. EBS, a partnership of the world's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market-making banks, handles an estimated 70% of the $1.2 trillion-a-day turnover in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the pound hit a new 11-year high of $1.8360 while the euro soared well over a cent to $1.2780, close to its all-time high of $1.2810. The dollar fell to CHF1.2245, but remained little changed against the yen at around Y106.15.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Volumes, Tactics Show Japan Means Business \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the same time, there's no denying that trading in the dollar against the yen is running into some problems of its own, principally because of the massive amount of dollars being bought by Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a bid to slow the appreciation of the yen and stop a strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from derailing its fragile economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In particular, the vigorous defense of the Y106.00 barrier by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be creating potential problems which could eventually spark even greater volatility down the line.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sheer magnitude of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the first trading days of this year - in excess of Y3 trillion, or $30 billion, according to dealers' estimates compared with about Y20 trillion in all of 2003- means some banks have filled their credit lines with the Japanese agent banks bidding for dollars on the finance ministry's behalf.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The huge supply of dollars in the market is being hoovered up by these Japanese agent banks so the spot rate isn't budging. But once the credit lines are hit, those banks can't deal with the Japanese banks, leaving them exposed with sell orders to execute.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the official Japanese bids - easily the biggest support for the dollar - are now holding it at the highest price in the market, the selling banks have no option but to trade with banks bidding at lower prices, exacerbating the downward pressure on the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such conditions don't "create an orderly market," said Enrico Caruso, dealer at Tempest Asset Management in Newport Beach, Calif, although he said there's no real risk of a crisis because of the double- and triple-A rated financial institutions that dominate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Caruso also points out that spot trades are cleared after only two days, so the rolling nature of these credit lines means there's little chance of them staying at those limits for long.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance ministry is also well aware of the problems. Dealers say it rotates the institution via which it places large bids for dollars in a bid to make sure it can absorb the dollar selling. "They really, really mean business this time," said the Japanese bank dealer in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040108e018000bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
