\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar wins respite, Snow and ECB comments awaited.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 14:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The dollar clawed back ground after the previous day's record low against the euro on Wednesday as the market waited to see if European and U.S. policymakers would signal any concern over exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also kept its balance against the yen, holding rigidly above the key 106 yen mark throughout the European morning amid wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers have so far not signalled any concern about the dollar's steep fall against the euro, which the market has taken as a green light to continue selling.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow is due to speak around 1600 GMT on Wednesday and ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet speaks after the bank's monetary policy meeting on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Will Trichet change his tone on the euro? The question he is also likely to be asked is at what level will they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "Comments will be more important than economic data in the next few days. Snow a few weeks ago saw no problem with the dollar, so maybe he'll give a hint."  \par \par \uc2 By 1245 GMT the dollar had recouped 1-1/2 cents from Tuesday's record low around $1.2810, to stand 0.4 percent stronger on the day at $1.2670 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market had been spooked into squaring short dollar positions after talk that a national \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Europe had sold euros above $1.28 on Tuesday, albeit for commercial rather than official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purposes, and that a well-known hedge fund had been selling euros earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was unchanged on the day at 106.17 yen having touched a three-year low this week just above 106.00 before suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled it up.  \par \par \uc2 POLICY SPEAK  \par \par \uc2 The euro has risen 10 percent against the dollar since early November and 20 percent over 2003 as a whole.  \par \par \uc2 While Germany's industry federation said the rise was hurting industry, Portugal's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was close to its historic average and ECB council member Ernst Welteke called the rise a market development central bankers could not influence.  \par \par \uc2 "No one is expecting a rate move from the ECB tomorrow but people are wondering if Trichet makes a comment about the euro which signals European policymakers are not as relaxed about its rise as they had been thinking," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London.  \par \par \uc2 "But all this could change rapidly with Snow speaking later."  \par \par \uc2 If Snow repeated his recent stance that the dollar's fall was orderly, the market would take that as a dollar sell-signal but a comment suggesting he was more concerned would be a catalyst for a dollar correction, Jalinoos said.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN CLEAR  \par \par \uc2 There was no such ambiguity regarding the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Traders in Tokyo suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen after it was detected in the market on Monday and Tuesday, but there was no confirmation of any action.  \par \par \uc2 A source told Reuters Japan spent around three trillion yen ($28 billion) in the first two days of this week to prevent an export-harming rise in the yen and stop it gaining beyond 106 per dollar. Japan spent a total 20 trillion yen throughout 2003 in keeping the yen in check.  \par \par \uc2 Although many Tokyo traders said it was a matter of time before the dollar broke through the 106-yen level, Japanese authorities were thought to be desperate to prevent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling into 105-yen territory.  \par \par \uc2 In Europe, EU Commission legal advisers said finance ministers acted illegally when they suspended the bloc's budget pact rules last year to spare France and Germany disciplinary action over their excessive deficits.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the news was another excuse to mark the euro down as anything which delayed changes to the pact was likely to be euro-negative.  \par \par \uc2 The euro shed 0.5 percent on the yen after hitting a five-month high in the previous session, while the dollar rebounded from multi-year lows against the Swiss franc, sterling and Australian and New Zealand dollars also set on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040108e0170010s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
