\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar gets reprieve, rises from low vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 22:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, changes dateline, previous CHICAGO)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The dollar rose on Wednesday from a record low against the euro as investors took a break from selling the greenback while paying scant attention to yet another 'strong dollar' statement by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow.  \par \par \uc2 Snow repeated the standard mantra of believing in a strong dollar to the U.S. Chamber of Commerce on Wednesday, and added the now familiar refrain that its value be set by the markets.  \par \par \uc2 But traders characterized the "strong dollar" policy, repeated ad nauseam over the years, as one of benign neglect.  \par \par \uc2 Over the past month the investors pushed the euro to record highs against the dollar practically every day, topping out at $1.2812 on Tuesday according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 "This market is still trying to digest the dollar's December swoon. The euro moved above $1.28 yesterday and this pullback is a correction. But it is a correction from such lofty levels and the dollar remains incredibly weak in relation to where it was six weeks ago," said Joe Francomano, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Erste Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell to a session low $1.2635, a loss of 0.74 percent on the day, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 The halt in the euro's rise is not expected to last long.  \par \par \uc2 "We've had such a rapid rise in the euro that I think it's reasonable to have a bit of a pause," said Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with J.P. Morgan Chase in New York. "I don't think there was anything fundamental that triggered it. I don't think this (pause) is going to last long."  \par \par \uc2 The backdrop for the dollar's decline is now well defined by the large and growing U.S. budget and current account deficits as well as 45-year-low benchmark U.S. interest rates which make dollar-based assets less appealing to foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday the International Monetary Fund said the growing deficits raise the risk of an abrupt drop in the value of the dollar which could hit U.S. and global economic growth, a risk that "cannot be ignored," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 "An abrupt weakening of investment sentiment vis-a-vis the dollar could possibly lead to adverse consequences both domestically and abroad," particularly since U.S. debts to the rest of the world are at record highs, the fund said.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar, barring a brief spike down to 105.92 yen JPY=) on what was thought to be a trading system malfunction, maintained a near steady level at 106.18 yen, unchanged from Tuesday's New York close, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 Caution ruled over yen trading as investors remain wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese financial authorities.  \par \par \uc2 Investors believe the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been in the market this week selling yen, even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not confirmed such action. Traders in Tokyo suspected the BOJ had continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in the Asian session after it was detected in the market on Monday and Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 A market source told Reuters Japan spent around three trillion yen ($28 billion) in the first two days of this week to prevent an export-harming rise in the yen and stop it gaining beyond 106 per dollar. Japan spent a total 20 trillion yen throughout 2003 in trying to keep the yen in check.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell 0.76 percent to 134.16 yen. The dollar gained 0.67 percent to 1.2392 Swiss francs. Sterling fell 0.18 percent to $1.8183.  \par \par \uc2 Traders now wait for comments due on Thursday from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, who will hold a news conference after the bank's monetary policy meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "Will Trichet change his tone on the euro? The question he is also likely to be asked is at what level will they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 While Germany's industry federation said the euro's rise was hurting, Portugal's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was close to its historic average. (Additional reporting by Kyle Peterson in Chicago and John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040107e017004ah\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
