\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rebounds in wait for ECB, US policymakers.  \b0\par\par\uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 12:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 By Charlotte Cooper LONDON, Jan 7 (Reuters) - The dollar clawed back ground after the previous day's record low against the euro on Wednesday as the market waited to see if European and U.S. policymakers would signal any concern over exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also kept its balance against the yen, holding above the key 106 yen mark amid wariness of a repeat of the previous day's suspected yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers have so far not signalled any concern about the dollar's steep fall against the euro, which the market has taken as a green light to continue selling.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow is due to speak later on Wednesday and ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet holds a news conference after the bank's monetary policy meeting on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Will Trichet change his tone on the euro? The question he is also likely to be asked is at what level will they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "Comments will be more important than economic data in the next few days. Snow a few weeks ago saw no problem with the dollar, so maybe he'll give a hint."  \par \par \uc2 By 1100 GMT the dollar had recouped 1-1/2 cents from Tuesday's record low around $1.2810, to stand half a percent stronger on the day at $1.2660.  \par \par \uc2 It was unchanged on the day at 106.15 yen having touched a three-year low this week just above 106.00 before suspected Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pulled it up.  \par \par \uc2 The euro shed half a percent on the yen after hitting a five-month high in the previous session, while the dollar rebounded from multi-year lows against the Swiss franc, sterling and Australian and New Zealand dollars also set on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 POLICY SPEAK  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said profit-taking on the euro's gains had set in with the market nervous of being caught out by a sharp correction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's acute rally but many doubted there would be a substantial shift in tone from policymakers yet.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has risen 10 percent just since early November. While Germany's industry federation said the rise was hurting industry, Portugal's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was close to its historic average.  \par \par \uc2 "No one is expecting a rate move from the ECB tomorrow but people are wondering if Trichet makes a comment about the euro which signals European policymakers are not as relaxed about its rise as they had been thinking," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London.  \par \par \uc2 "But all this could change rapidly with Snow speaking later."  \par \par \uc2 If Snow repeated his recent stance that the dollar's fall was orderly and not undervalued, the market would take that as a dollar sell-signal but a comment suggesting he was more concerned would be a catalyst for a dollar correction, Jalinoos said.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN CLEAR  \par \par \uc2 There was no such ambiguity regarding the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Traders in Tokyo suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in the Asian session after it was detected in the market on Monday and Tuesday, but there was no confirmation of any action.  \par \par \uc2 A source told Reuters Japan spent around three trillion yen ($28 billion) in the first two days of this week to prevent an export-harming rise in the yen and stop it gaining beyond 106 per dollar. Japan spent a total 20 trillion yen throughout 2003 in keeping the yen in check.  \par \par \uc2 Although many Tokyo traders said it was a matter of time before the dollar broke through the 106-yen level, Japanese authorities were thought to be desperate to prevent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling into 105-yen territory.  \par \par \uc2 In Europe, debate over ballooning EU budget deficits continued, with EU Commission legal advisers saying finance ministers acted illegally when they suspended the bloc's budget rules last year to spare France and Germany disciplinary action over their excessive deficits.  \par \par \uc2 The Commission will debate next week whether to mount a legal challenge to the suspension. Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market wants to see the deficit rules changed and anything which delayed that was likely to be euro-negative.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040107e017002l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
