\par\par \b \uc2 EU Officials Concerned, But Powerless Over Dollar Decline  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Pierre Briancon  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 14:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 PARIS (Dow Jones)--The rapidly declining dollar and the economic pain it could cause in Europe have become hot topics at cabinet meetings and among central bankers, but European authorities say their options to alleviate it are severely limited.  \par \par \uc2 A senior French government official, speaking on condition of anonymity, said earlier this week that with few policy options to turn to, there's little chance finance ministers or other euro-zone government officials will publicly step into the dispute about how or whether to cool the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Economists say a move by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower interest rates would unlikely be big or dramatic enough to slow the dollar's decline, let alone turn it around. Hanging over everything is the U.S.'s deficit situation.  \par \par \uc2 "Due to the twin deficits, there still is a strong decline potential in the dollar against all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Patrick Artus, head of economic research at French bank CDC-Ixis. "Cutting euro interest rates from 2% to 1.5% wouldn't do much against this long-term trend."  \par \par \uc2 The French government official added there's also little public pressure on European governments to act, since "there is surprisingly little protest (among European industrialists) against the current level of the euro."  \par \par \uc2 Most major companies have hedged against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, he said. "And the biggest chunk of the member countries' trade is done within the euro-zone, so the consequences of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  variations are limited by the euro's mere existence."  \par \par \uc2 German automaker, Porsche AG (POR3.XE), for example, this week said it had hedged all of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure through mid-2006. Porsche expects to sell about 70,000 cars in the current business year, half of them in the U.S., meaning heavy dollar exposure. Porsche's business year ends July 31.  \par \par \uc2 Even without big hedging, "the current euro level still leaves room for the German economy to demonstrate its competitiveness," said a German government source, who also declined to be named commenting on what's seen as a sensitive subject.  \par \par \uc2 "It has already demonstrated this ability last year," the source said, referring to an estimated 2% export growth in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists pointed out that the strong growth of world trade for now is offsetting the effects of the rising euro on European exporters.  \par \par \uc2 But, "this only means that without the dollar decline, European exporters would do even better," Artus insisted.  \par \par \uc2 Germany, he says, is the euro-zone country taking the most advantage of growth in world trade, "but they're still on a mere 5% growth trend for their exports," he added.  \par \par \uc2 As the euro - currently at $1.2669 and nearing the $1.30 mark seen by some economists as a pain threshold for the E.U. economy - government officials have largely stuck to their official "no comment" line about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest-rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB governing council will meet Thursday to decide on euro-zone interest rates and update its 2004 economic projections.  \par \par \uc2 Most economists agree the only way to correct the current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  imbalance would be through coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by major central banks.  \par \par \uc2 Christian de Boissieu, head of French Prime Minister Jean-Pierre Raffarin's Economic Analysis Council, argued in an article in La Tribune Wednesday that the ECB should coordinate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other Asian central banks.  \par \par \uc2 But Artus said only the U.S. Federal Reserve's cooperation would make \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  effective.  \par \par \uc2 "And as long as inflation doesn't pick up in the U.S., he added, it is not in the U.S.'s interest to stop the dollar decline."  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, some government and ECB officials have privately begun to signal a shift in their benign neglect policy towards the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 A senior ECB official was quoted Dec. 29th by the Financial Times as saying the dollar's fall had been "relatively sharp," and that risks induced by the U.S. trade and budget deficits were "now materializing."  \par \par \uc2 "Up to now, the ECB could be criticized for being too explicit in its, 'we don't care' policy, offering a one-way bet to \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," said the French government official, adding that "a bit more fuzziness" was needed on the subject.  \par \par \uc2 Other ECB policy makers echoed the same preoccupation, with the Belgian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor quoted Dec. 26 in De Morgen as saying that a new, strong appreciation of the euro was "not desirable."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was worth $1.24 on that day, and has since risen sharply. The latest ECB projections, published in December, were based on a euro at $1.17.  \par \par \uc2 By Pierre Briancon, Dow Jones Newswires; +331 4017 1740; pierre.briancon@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Andrea Thomas in Berlin contributed to this report  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040107e017000iq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
