\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Japan Economist: Dlr May Fall To Y90 By 2008  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 09:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar's recent weakness against the yen is part of a long-term correction that could take the U.S. unit down as low as Y90 in the next few years, a prominent Japanese economist said recently.  \par \par \uc2 Structural problems, chiefly the massive U.S. trade and budget deficits, will weigh on the greenback for several years, preventing any major dollar rally even with the help of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Susumu Takahashi, chief economist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Research Institute, a major private think tank.  \par \par \uc2 "For the U.S. to cover its massive trade deficit through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment alone, the dollar would have to fall as low as Y70," Takahashi said in a recent interview with Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 "This would be unacceptable for Japan and the U.S. since the yen's rise to such a level would cause severe damage to both of their economies," Takahashi said. "The two nations will instead try to adjust the imbalance through a more gradual drop of the dollar." Even so, Takahashi expects the U.S. unit to slide to around Y90 by 2008.  \par \par \uc2 For 2004, Takahashi expects the dollar to head down toward Y100 by June before bouncing back to around Y105 by year-end. The pace of the yen's rise will slow somewhat in the latter half of the year due to an expected slowdown in the Japanese economy, he said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0815 GMT Wednesday, the dollar was trading at Y106.22.  \par \par \uc2 Although the U.S. economy enjoys robust growth, financial markets are seizing on the massive current-account gap, which needs to be financed with about $2 billion of foreign money every business day.  \par \par \uc2 Receding hopes of an early U.S. interest rate hike continue to dampen global investors' appetite for U.S. assets, raising concern over whether the U.S. will be able to attract enough global funds to continue financing its massive deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Takahashi blamed the huge trade deficit on "too strong growth" in the U.S. economy compared with that of Japan and European nations, saying this gap is boosting imports to the U.S. and creating a huge global trade imbalance.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. wants Japan and other major economies to shoulder the costs of wiping out the imbalance by allowing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "This was the basic idea behind the Group of Seven meeting in Dubai last year, and the G-7 economies will likely stick to this stance" when their finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs meet next month in Florida, Takahashi said.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar-selling for yen gathered steam after the G-7 nations called for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility at the Dubai meeting - code for Japan and other Asian export powerhouses to stop weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Takahashi, however, expects \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent a sharp yen rise from hurting the nation's nascent economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo could easily spend about the same amount in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year as the record total of more than Y20 trillion it spent to that end in 2003, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The MOF knows that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't have much effect in halting the dollar's slide, but it will act anyway to at least slow the pace of decline," Takahashi said.  \par \par \uc2 "And the U.S. will tolerate \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as long as they're aimed at smoothing sharp moves" and not at intentionally cheapening the yen, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF fears that a stronger yen would hurt Japan's export-driven economic recovery since it makes Japanese goods more expensive or less profitable overseas and crimps the yen value of Japanese companies' foreign earnings.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040107e0170009s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw174\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2610\pichgoal600
