\par\par \b \uc2 Business in Asia Today -- Jan. 7, 2004  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,111 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2004\par 13:09\par \uc2 PR Newswire\par \uc2 PRN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004 PR Newswire Association LLC.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Australia, Jan. 7 /PRNewswire/ -- A summary prepared by Asia Pulse ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.asiapulse.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.asiapulse.com/}}}\uc2 ), the real-time, Asia-based wire with exclusive news, market intelligence and business opportunities:  \par \par \uc2 CHINA REPORTS FY COMMUNICATIONS SERVICES INCOME OF US$62 BLN  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING - The income earned by China's communications services sector reached 515 billion yuan (about US$62 billion) in 2003, up 12.8 per cent over the figure for the previous year, according to the latest statistics from the Ministry of Information Industry. More than 90 per cent of the income came from telecommunications, which rose 13.9 per cent over the previous year. In 2003, the number of phone users in China grew by 112 million to reach 532 million - the largest number of phone users in the world.  \par \par \uc2 RECORD INVESTMENT FROM TAIWAN FLOWS INTO EAST CHINA PROVINCE  \par \par \uc2 NANCHANG - East China's Jiangxi Province registered 122 Taiwan-funded projects in 2003, using a record US$722 million of investment from Taiwan, a rise of 107 per cent on-year. The big increase was largely attributed to a local fair in 2003 which attracted investment from Taiwan, said Liu Yansong, an official in charge of Taiwan-funded businesses. More than 600 businesspeople from Taiwan attended the fair and expressed great interest in investing in Jiangxi, Liu said.  \par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIAN WITNESSES RECORD NEW VEHICLE SALES IN 2003  \par \par \uc2 ADELAIDE - New vehicle sales in Australia would again top the 900,000 mark this year following a record 2003, the Federal Chamber of Automotive Industries said today. Australians purchased 909,811 vehicles last year, surpassing the previous annual record of 824,309 in 2002. Chamber chief executive Peter Sturrock said sales records were posted every month last year. Toyota was the top selling automotive brand in Australia in 2003, with 186,370 new vehicle sales, or 20.5 per cent of the market.  \par \par \uc2 XINJIANG TO MAKE HANDSOME INVESTMENT IN ROAD CONSTRUCTION  \par \par \uc2 URUMQI - Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region in northwestern China is expected to put 14 billion yuan (US$1.7 billion) into road construction in 2004, a significant rise from 10 billion yuan (US$1.2 billion) in 2003. The region has built more than 82,000 km of roads to date, 1,645 km of which were newly built or re-built in 2003, according to a source with the local communications department. From 2001-2003, the region invested a total of 20.2 billion yuan (US$2.42 billion) in road construction.  \par \par \uc2 JAPANESE GOVT \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENES\b0 \highlight\uc2  TO THWART DOLLAR'S FALL VS YEN  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Tuesday for a second straight day in a bid to curb a further rise in the yen. The yen reached a 40-month high against the dollar in Tokyo trading amid strong demand from overseas investors. With the dollar falling to 106.09 yen at one point, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Japan determined that the breach of the 106 yen mark would send the dollar into free fall, and quickly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO TATEMONO 03 GROUP PRETAX PROFIT SEEN RISING 25 PCT  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO - Tokyo Tatemono Co.'s group pretax profit for the year through December 31 is expected to be about 25 per cent higher than the previous year's figure, rising to about 14 billion yen (US$132 million) to top its projection of around 13.3 billion yen. Group sales have been estimated at 130 billion yen, a 6 per cent increase. Revenue from the company's office rental business fell slightly, but the decline was more than offset by stronger sales of condominiums and homes.  \par \par \uc2 DAEWOO HEAVY WINS US$35 MLN IN MACHINE TOOL ORDERS FROM CHINA  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL - South Korea's Daewoo Heavy Industries & Machinery Co. said it has won US$35 million in machine tool orders from Chinese auto parts makers, accelerating its advance into the rapidly growing Chinese market. Daewoo Heavy said its latest machine tool orders came from three Chinese auto parts makers that are seeking to build new processing and assembly lines at their plants. The auto parts-related machine tools will be supplied to the firms on a turnkey basis, it added.  \par \par \uc2 KOREA'S KOOKMIN BANK REMAINS OPPOSED TO RESCUE PLAN FOR LG CARD  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL - Kookmin Bank , South Korea's largest lender, said it is still opposed to a creditor plan to co-manage ailing LG Card Co. . Kookmin has been given until late this afternoon to decide whether to accept the plan. Kookmin is the most vocal opponent of the co-management plan for LG Card, the nation's largest credit card issuer which is saddled with debts of more than 23 trillion won (US$19.34 billion).  \par \par \uc2 AUSTRALIAN HOME BUILDING APPROVALS FALL 6.7 PCT IN NOVEMBER  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA - Home building approvals fell by 6.7 per cent in November following the Reserve Bank of Australia raising interest rates for the first time in 17 months, new figures revealed today. The Australian Bureau of Statistics seasonally adjusted figures are the first since the bank tightened monetary policy with the first of two 0.25 per cent increases in the official interest rate. While the seasonally adjusted estimate for total dwelling approvals fell 6.7 per cent to 14,913 for the month, houses fell by only 1.2 per cent to 10,252.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE'S MANUFACTURING ACTIVITY EXPANDS IN DECEMBER  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE - Singapore's manufacturing activity expanded at its fastest rate in 36 months in December, led by increases in new orders, imports, employment and production output. The Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) stood at 54.5 in December, up 1.3 points from November, the Institute of Purchasing & Materials Management said. A reading above 50 indicates an expansion in activity, while a reading below 50 shows a contraction. The index has held above the key 50 threshold for seven consecutive months.  \par \par \uc2 Asia Pulse Pte Ltd. Each day Asia Pulse creates up to 300 items of news, business opportunities, expert commentary and industry profiles. Asia Pulse is a unique joint venture involving the resources of Asia's major news and information groups:  \par \par \uc2 \f2  (AAP) - AAP Information Services Pty Ltd (Australia)\par  (ANTARA) - LKBN ANTARA (Indonesia)\par  (CNA) - Central News Agency (Taiwan)\par  (IRNA)- Islamic Republic News Agency (Iran)\par  (Nikkei) - Nihon Keizai Shimbun Inc (Japan)\par  (ONA) - Oman News Agency (Oman)\par  (PNA) - Philippines News Agency (Philippines)\par  (PPI) - Pakistan Press International (Pakistan)\par  (PTI) - The Press Trust of India Ltd (India)\par  (Yonhap) - Yonhap News Agency (Korea)\par  (VNA) - Vietnam News Agency (Vietnam)\par  (XIC) - Xinhua Information Centre (China)\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 CONTACT: Asia Pulse Production Centre, in Sydney, Australia,+61-29322-8634 or email,  \uc2 http://www.apulse.com/+61-29322-8634\uc2  or email,  \par \par \uc2 Dokument PRN0000020040107e01700335\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
