\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Euro bulls test pain threshold as $1.30 looms.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2004\par 16:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 6 (Reuters) - The euro's surge against the tumbling dollar has so far been greeted remarkably calmly by European policymakers, leaving traders speculating how much higher it needs to go before authorities are goaded into action.  \par \par \uc2 The euro leapt above $1.28 on Tuesday, bringing its rise to more than 15 percent in the past four months and 40 percent in the past two years.  \par \par \uc2 On current form, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have already punched above $1.30 by the time Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers meet in Florida in early February - a level a G7 source told Reuters last week represented Europe's "pain threshold".  \par \par \uc2 Business groups have already lamented the euro's export-crimping rise but European politicians and central bankers have largely played down these concerns, noting the euro is not far off its long-term average.  \par \par \uc2 However, an acceleration above the psychological $1.30 level could prompt a change in tone, fuelling calls for euro zone policymakers to cut interest rates or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 "A sustained move above $1.30 will concern the ECB as it will prompt downward revisions to growth forecasts," said Neil MacKinnon, chief economist at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt management group ECU.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar's decline will definitely feature at the February G7 meeting. The bottom line is whether it has reached the point of triggering a policy response."  \par \par \uc2 POLICY OPTIONS  \par \par \uc2 The G7 meeting in Boca Raton, Florida, on February 6-7 will provide a crucial test of how far and how fast policymakers are willing to let the dollar fall and could be a golden opportunity for a coordinated policy response - if enough countries agree.  \par \par \uc2 However, given the export benefits of a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say the United States is unlikely to back steps to stem the dollar's slide in an election year unless turbulence spills into asset markets, threatening recovery.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is usually a last resort and only used if other options, such as verbal warnings or interest rate moves, have failed.  \par \par \uc2 European policymakers' first line of defence is likely to be to encourage Asian governments to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise against the dollar, allowing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline to be shouldered more broadly.  \par \par \uc2 But this was already the aim of September's G7 communique which called for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility and which backfired against the euro as dollar bears sold the greenback against any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  they could.  \par \par \uc2 "The problem is that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains pegged to the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so the euro is under exaggerated pressure," said Brian Hilliard, director of economic research at Societe Generale.  \par \par \uc2 GOING IT ALONE?  \par \par \uc2 Given likely resistance to coordinated dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the United States and foot-dragging by China on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation, European policymakers may have to consider a more unilateral approach.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has already taken action to retain export competitiveness, throwing 20 trillion yen ($188 billion) at the foreign exchanges in the past year - a move that slowed the yen's advance against the dollar to 10 percent over 2003, half the gain racked up by the euro.  \par \par \uc2 However, a tactic of pouring a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  onto the market is not only diplomatically difficult but also potentially inflationary. This approach could be difficult for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when euro zone inflation remains above target.  \par \par \uc2 Figures this week showed inflation in the euro zone eased slightly to 2.1 percent in December but remained above the ECB's two percent tolerance threshold.  \par \par \uc2 "Sticky inflation in the euro zone makes it harder for the ECB to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cut rates, so the point of policy response may be some way off," said Michael Metcalfe, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at State Street.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan is not worried about fuelling inflation so there is no problem there, but the market knows the ECB could not afford to take such action for very long."  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts expect the ECB would sooner cut interest rates that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the foreign exchanges.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations of rate hikes in the euro zone have already been significantly reduced as the euro has rallied and German think tank DIW said on Tuesday that a rise in the euro to $1.35 would need to be offset by a one percentage point cut in interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040106e016003i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
