\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2  to smooth, not halt, dollar fall.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2004\par 09:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 6 (Reuters) - The Japanese authorities have the funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again this year but analysts say they won't reverse the dollar's fall and may have to be satisfied with simply smoothing its descent.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the widening U.S. current deficit is the main factor behind the broad-based dollar weakness and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do nothing to change that.  \par \par \uc2 "They could spend nine to 10 trillion yen ($84.7-$94.1 billion) a month in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...but it will be very difficult to turn around the current trend unless some dollar-buying factors emerge," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 Like other economists, he felt that Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be aimed at slowing the speed of the dollar's fall rather than a probably futile attempt to reverse the trend.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a three-year low of 106.06 yen on Monday - down almost 10 percent since mid-September when it began its latest slide. At the beginning of last year, it was around 119 yen.  \par \par \uc2 To slow the yen's rise and limit the negative impact on the export-led Japanese economy, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) spent a record 20 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 - equivalent to $188 billion at current rates - and is believed to have begun 2004 in the same way, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both days this week.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Toyota Motor Corp President Fujio Cho repeated his view that the yen was "a bit too strong" in the light of Japan's economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 But Japanese companies are living with the strong yen and profits in general are rising. Some economists say that last year's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could therefore be termed a success in that it ironed out violent fluctuations and gave firms time to adjust.  \par \par \uc2 PASSIVE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  EXPECTED  \par \par \uc2 In a move to secure more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, the MOF announced last month that it would seek to raise the borrowing limit on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  account to 140 trillion yen in the fiscal year starting next April from 79 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 That massive increase of 61 trillion yen is equivalent to about $540 billion - roughly the annualised size of the U.S. current account deficit based on latest third quarter figures.  \par \par \uc2 As a stopgap measure to avoid a funding crunch before parliament approves the raising of the borrowing limit, the ministry has arranged a 'repo' deal with the Bank of Japan in which it can get funds by selling foreign bonds to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying the paper back at a later date.  \par \par \uc2 But most analysts think that the Japanese authorities will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively since the current weakness of the dollar is not just limited to the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Since mid-September, the dollar has dropped about 17 percent against the Australian dollar and close to 15 percent versus the euro, touching a record low of $1.2707 per euro on Tuesday. "If such action (aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push up the dollar) is taken, international pressure against Japan could increase," said Junya Tanase, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 "So any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be aimed at slowing the dollar's fall and not pushing it up."  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts see 105 yen as a key defence line for the Japanese authorities since a break through that level is likely to prompt heavy option-related selling, which could rapidly push the dollar down to around 102 yen.  \par \par \uc2 But JP Morgan's Tanase felt the ministry would be flexible, especially if Japanese share prices and the economy continued to show signs of recovery despite the strong yen.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think they (MOF) have a defence line. They will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the strong yen is hurting the economy and will loosen their stance if they see that it isn't doing much harm," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Both Tanase and Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Fukaya expect the dollar to fall as low as 102 yen by the end of March.  \par \par \uc2 Tanase then expects the dollar to go under 100 yen by September, but Fukaya doesn't think it will fall that far.  \par \par \uc2 "I expect to see some recovery in the dollar from April," he said. ($1=106.26 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040106e016002bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
