\par\par \b \uc2 Stocks Close Mainly Higher, With Dow Dragging Behind  \b0\par\par\uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2004\par 23:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Erin Schulte \par The Wall Street Journal Online   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wall Street shifted into neutral Tuesday after gunning to new 22-month highs a session earlier, with disappointing economic data dousing enthusiasm.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 A warning from Gateway weighed on the computer maker's shares.  \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gave up 5.41 points to 10538.65 a day after hitting its highest level since March 2002. The Nasdaq Composite Index rose 10.01 to 2057.37, while the S&P 500-stock index gained 1.45 to 1123.67.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say a pullback has been expected for some time, but they don't think it will be too severe.  \par \par \uc2 "Many of the indicators that we use to try to predict short-term stock market moves suggest a market that has come too far, too fast, or, in other words, is overbought," said Rod Smyth, chief investment strategist at Wachovia Securities. "Any stock-market pullback will be relatively mild since we expect good first-quarter earnings on the back of a strong economy and we do not expect the Fed to alter its accommodative policy in the first quarter."  \par \par \uc2 He thinks the S&P 500 will eventually find support at around 1060, about a 5% decline from its current level.  \par \par \uc2 In economic news, demand for manufactured goods slid in November, as orders for computers and electronic products took their biggest drop in more than three years. U.S. factory orders fell 1.4% to $336.91 billion, the Commerce Department said, less than the 1.5% drop economists had expected.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Institute for Supply Management's December nonmanufacturing index, which measures the health of the service sector, stood at 58.6. Any number above 50 indicates expansion, but economists had foreseen a reading of 60.5 and the number was lower than November's reading of 60.1.  \par \par \uc2 Among stocks to watch, Gateway shares sank 13% after the Poway, Calif., company -- which has struggled for three years to halt a rapid decline in its sales -- said fourth-quarter revenue would be about $880 million, below the $925 million to $975 million it estimated in October. A year ago, it reported sales of $1.1 billion. The company also said it would lose 15 cents a share for the quarter, excluding restructuring costs.  \par \par \uc2 SoundView analyst Michelle L. Gutierrez downgraded the stock to "neutral" from "outperform," saying the revenue estimate shows "no discernable seasonal benefit." She is also concerned about Gateway's ability to "succeed in the competitive consumer-electronics arena as well as management's ability to execute on its restructuring plan."  \par \par \uc2 Starbucks edged up 1.2% after it raised its first-quarter earnings target and said December same-store sales climbed 11% from a year ago. It expects to earn 25 cents to 26 cents a share for the first quarter, higher than Wall Street's 24 cents a share estimate and last year's earnings of 20 cents.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar continued to struggle against its rivals in overseas trading. The euro surged to new highs against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, while the strengthening yen raised the prospect of another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, some analysts cautioned the dollar could be oversold and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might warn against overzealous purchasing of the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when board members meet on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 In major U.S. market action:  \par \par \uc2 Stocks were weak. On the Big Board, where 1.49 billion shares changed hands, 1,724 issues rose and 1,579 fell. On the Nasdaq, where 2.27 billion shares traded, 1,808 advanced and 1,409 declined.  \par \par \uc2 Bonds gained. The 10-year Treasury rose about more than 3/4 point, or $7.50 for each $1,000 invested. The yield, which moves inversely to price, fell to 4.279%. The 30-year Treasury bond tacked on more than a point, putting its yield at 5.107%.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was weaker. It slipped to 106.20 yen from 106.27 yen late Monday in New York, while the euro rose to $1.2739 from $1.2657.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040106e0160003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
