\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits record low vs euro after Bernanke.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2004\par 17:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Jan 5 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a record low against the euro on Monday after a Federal Reserve official said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is right to hold interest rates at 45-year lows even though the U.S. economy has improved.  \par \par \uc2 The prospect of interest rates remaining low is likely to encourage the outflow of funds from the United States to higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when the United States needs capital inflows to cover its current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. external shortfall - a major worry of international investors - is also likely to come under growing pressure because economic recovery could lead to more imports.  \par \par \uc2 "It's fairly dovish," Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago, said of comments on Sunday by Fed Governor Ben Bernanke. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market right now is looking for some type of a signal to stop shorting the dollar. What he's saying is the signal is going to be a long time in coming."  \par \par \uc2 Bernanke told the American Economic Association in San Diego: "For now, I believe that the Federal Reserve has the luxury of being patient."  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's Open Markets Committee is scheduled to meet to discuss interest rates in late January and markets are forecasting U.S. rates to remain on hold then.  \par \par \uc2 Bernanke also said it was a mistake just to look at the dollar's sharp drop against the euro, noting that the greenback's fall against a broad basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been much smaller. He said the risk of a "dollar crisis" was low.  \par \par \uc2 In mid-morning New York trading, the euro was up 0.71 percent at $1.2668 . The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier traded at a new lifetime high of $1.2695, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was down 0.51 percent at 1.2323 Swiss francs . Sterling gained 0.73 percent to $1.8058 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was down 0.78 percent at 106.18 yen after trading at a new three-year low of 106.25 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "We have an economy growing quite nicely, but the Fed is showing no intention of hiking rates," said Hans Guenter Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London. "We have had some strong data from the U.S., but Bernanke's comments were the most important thing. So the dollar weakening trend is continuing."  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economic data schedule featured little to move \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. November construction spending increased more than expected to a record high, data showed. Overall construction spending jumped 1.2 percent, but markets took little interest in the news.  \par \par \uc2 At 12:40 p.m. EST(1740 GMT), Atlanta Fed President Jack Guynn speaks on the 2004 economic outlook.  \par \par \uc2 Markets were also focusing on comments from policy-makers about dollar weakness ahead of this weekend's bi-monthly meeting of central bankers at the Bank for International Settlements in Switzerland and next month's meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrialized nations.  \par \par \uc2 A G7 source told Reuters the group will look at the weakened dollar at its Feb. 6-7 meeting. But analysts said the key to the meeting would be the stance of Washington, which has so far kept mum on the weak dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said Japanese and speculative names were selling dollars for yen even though the market was wary of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after suspected yen selling in Asian trading.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice finance minister for international affairs, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Monday Japan remained ready to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if there was a danger of volatility.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Justyna Pawlak in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040105e01500393\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
