\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sinks again, Bernanke deals fresh blow.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2004\par 14:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 5 (Reuters) - The euro rose to a life high against the dollar, which hit a three-year low on the yen on Monday after a top Federal Reserve official reinforced views that U.S. interest rates would remain low for some time.  \par \par \uc2 Investors, already eager to sell dollars, seized on comments from Fed Governor Ben Bernanke as an excuse to push the dollar broadly lower. He said on Sunday that the Fed was right to hold rates at 45-year lows given low inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The prospect of interest rates remaining low is expected to encourage investment outflows from the United States to higher yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when the U.S. capital inflows are needed to cover the country's current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. external shortfall - a major worry of international investors - is also likely to be under growing pressure as economic recovery could lead to higher imports.  \par \par \uc2 "People are latching on to anything that's negative and Bernanke certainly didn't help. There is no sign of this ending," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank in London. "The next target is $1.2725."  \par \par \uc2 The greenback fell as low as $1.2695 per euro, before paring losses to $1.2676 by 1245 GMT. It hit seven-year lows against the Swiss franc below 1.2300, 11-year lows versus sterling and seven-year lows against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also stumbled to a three-year low of 106.25 yen, breaking down through support around 106.60/70 yen which had underpinned the dollar for the past month.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said Japanese and speculative names were selling dollars for yen even though the market was wary of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after suspected yen selling action in Asian trading hours. "We think the BoJ (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  a couple of times but couldn't stand the tide," said Rabobank's Ferridge.  \par \par \uc2 POLICYMAKERS STAND PAT, MOSTLY  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's Open Markets Committee is due to meet to discuss interest rates in late January and markets were forecasting U.S. rates to remain on hold then.  \par \par \uc2 "We have an economy growing quite nicely but the Fed is showing no intention of hiking rates," said Hans Guenter Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London. "So the dollar weakening trend is continuing."  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) was also expected to stand pat this week as inflation remains near its target range, holding its official borrowing costs at 2.0 percent against U.S. rates of 1.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The flash estimate of December inflation in the euro zone showed consumer prices rising 2.1 percent on the year, compared with 2.2 percent in November.  \par \par \uc2 Markets were also focusing on comments from policymakers about dollar weakness ahead of this weekend's bi-monthly meeting of central bankers at the Bank for International Settlements in Switzerland and next month's meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrialised nations.  \par \par \uc2 A G7 source has told Reuters the group will look at the weakened dollar at its February 6-7 meeting. But analysts said key to the meeting would be the stance of Washington, which has so far kept mum on the falling dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Keeping true to Washington's line, Bernanke also said it was a mistake just to look at the dollar's sharp drop against the euro, and the greenback's fall against a broad basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been much smaller.  \par \par \uc2 He said the risk of a "dollar crisis" was low.  \par \par \uc2 However, analysts said Japan was likely to protest against the yen's rise versus the dollar. Last year Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($187 billion) - more than Japan's current account surplus of around 15 trillion yen - to stem an export-damaging rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But that massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in part offset by foreign investors buying yen to purchase rising Japanese shares. Traders expect such flows to continue this year.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040105e015002rk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
