\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits fresh lows on euro, yen after Bernanke.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2004\par 12:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 5 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a record low versus the euro on Monday and a three-year low on the yen after a top Federal Reserve official reinforced views that U.S. interest rates would remain low for some time.  \par \par \uc2 Fed Governor Ben Bernanke said late on Sunday that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was right to hold interest rates at 45-year lows given the low rate of inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The prospect of interest rates remaining low is expected to encourage investment outflows from the U.S. to other higher yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when the U.S. needs capital inflows to cover its current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. external shortfall - a major worry of international investors - is also likely to be under growing pressure as economic recovery could lead to higher imports.  \par \par \uc2 "We have an economy growing quite nicely but the Fed is showing no intention of hiking rates," said Hans Guenter Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London.  \par \par \uc2 "We have had some strong data from the U.S. but Bernanke's comments were the most important thing. So the dollar weakening trend is continuing."  \par \par \uc2 The greenback fell as low as $1.2695 per euro, before paring losses to $1.2664 by 1100 GMT. It hit seven-year lows against the Swiss franc below 1.2300 and against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The dollar also stumbled to a three-year low against the yen, breaking down through support around 106.60/70 yen which has underpinned the dollar for the past month.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said Japanese and speculative names were selling dollars for yen even though the market was wary of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after suspected yen selling action in Asian trading hours.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice finance minister for international affairs, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Monday Japan remained ready to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if there was a danger of volatility.  \par \par \uc2 POLICYMAKERS STAND PAT, MOSTLY  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's Open Markets Committee is due to meet to discuss interest rates in late January and markets were forecasting U.S. rates to remain on hold then.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also expected to stand pat this week as inflation remains near its target range, holding its official borrowing costs at 2.0 percent or at twice as much as U.S. rates at 1.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The flash estimate of December inflation in the euro zone showed consumer prices running at 2.1 percent on the year compared with 2.2 percent in November.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, markets were also focusing on comments from policymakers about dollar weakness ahead of this weekend's bi-monthly meeting of central bankers at the Bank for International Settlements in Switzerland and next month's meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrialised nations.  \par \par \uc2 A G7 source has told Reuters the group will look at the weakened dollar at its February 6-7 meeting. But analysts said key to the meeting would the stance of Washington, which has so far kept mum on the weak dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Keeping true to Washington's line, Bernanke also said it was a mistake just to look at the dollar's sharp drop against the euro, adding the greenback's fall against a broad basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been much smaller.  \par \par \uc2 He said the risk of a "dollar crisis" was low.  \par \par \uc2 However, analysts said Japan was likely to be the loudest to protest against the yen's rise against the dollar. Last year Japan sold a record 20 trillion yen ($187 billion) - more than Japan's current account surplus of around 15 trillion yen - to stem an export-damaging rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But that massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in part offset by foreign investors buying yen to purchase Japanese shares. Traders expect such flows to continue this year. The Nikkei average ended a shortened half-day session up 1.39 percent on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040105e015002ab\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
