\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits record low vs euro, Fed comments weigh.  \b0\par\par\uc2 368 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2004\par 02:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 5 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a record low against the euro on Monday as Tokyo traders returned from New Year holidays in a mood to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on worries about the widening U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said comments by Federal Reserve Governor Ben Bernanke were also pressuring the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Bernanke, in remarks prepared for delivery at a meeting of the American Economic Association in San Diego on Sunday, said that while the U.S. economy appeared to have turned a corner, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was right to hold interest rates at 45-year lows given the low rate of underlying inflation.  \par \par \uc2 By 0047 GMT, the euro was at a record high of $1.2672 , above the previous high of 1.2649 hit last week and 1.2585/89 in late U.S. trade on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling rose to an 11-year high of around $1.7961 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading at 106.91/96 yen compared with 107.04/12 in late New York.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the dollar hit a session low of 106.90 yen. Traders dismissed a deal done around 106.70 as a punching error.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar was also bruised by a rise in Tokyo share prices. The Nikkei average was up more than one percent in early business on the first trading day of the year.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar continues to be weighed and a rise in the Nikkei should be another reason for the market to cap any gains," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 But the market remained wary of possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data last week showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about 20 trillion yen ($187 billion) in 2003 to stem an export-damaging rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Monday that Japanese authorities remained ready to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if there was a danger of volatility.  \par \par \uc2 "We will take appropriate measures when necessary. There is no change in our position" Mizoguchi, vice finance minister for international affairs, told reporters as he arrived for work. ($1=106.99 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040105e015001h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
