\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Hits Lows on Lack of Official Concern  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,058 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2004\par 22:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar tumbled across the board Monday as the first full week of 2004 got underway, setting a fresh all-time low against the euro, and hitting a three-year low against the yen despite apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The negative tone was set over the weekend, when Federal Reserve Governor Ben Bernanke said the chances of a dollar crisis are "quite low" and that interest rates will likely be held down for some time.  \par \par \uc2 With U.S. interest rates at 45-year lows, bond markets are finding it increasingly difficult to attract enough foreign investment to finance the massive U.S. current account deficits and keep the dollar from falling.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Bernanke's comments gave "traders the green light to keep selling the dollar," said Omer Esiner, market analyst at Ruesch International in Washington. "(Bernanke) reiterated the market's outlook, not only for steady interest rates out of the U.S., but also the fact that U.S. officials are more amenable to an orderly dollar decline."  \par \par \uc2 Despite the euro's nearly 20% rise against the dollar in 2003, the trade-weighted U.S. dollar index remains above the historical average, noted Mr. Bernanke. He also said it would be in the interests of U.S. trading partners to avoid a crisis.  \par \par \uc2 After a brief reprieve Friday from the heavy selling pressure of recent months, the dollar hit a new record low against the euro around $1.2694 and fell to multiyear lows against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well.  \par \par \uc2 Monday afternoon, the euro was trading around $1.2664, up from $1.2585 late Friday in New York. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also at 134.45 yen, below 134.63 yen. Sterling rose to a new 11-year high around $1.8104 before settling at $1.8060, versus $1.7930 Friday. After hitting a seven-year low against the Swiss franc of 1.2286 franc, the dollar was trading at 1.2337 franc, down from 1.2377 franc Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also fell to a 10-year low against the Canadian dollar and six-year lows against the Australian and New Zealand dollars.  \par \par \uc2 But some of the biggest drama of the session came against the yen, which fell to a three-year low of 106.07 yen despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. It was down from 106.95 yen late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was estimated by traders to have bought $3 billion to $5 billion to stem the dollar's slide versus \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The first apparent action by the BOJ in Tokyo trade sent the dollar as high as 107.36 yen, but selling pressure eventually sent it to a low of 106.07 yen, despite the continued presence of official bids, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Last year, Japanese authorities sold a record 20.05 trillion yen to try to keep the yen competitively weak for Japanese exporters. The Finance Ministry is gearing up for a bigger battle against yen strength by bolstering reserve funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Right now, many market participants believe only official action by monetary authorities - or at least strong "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " - can slow the dollar's slide. But while the rhetoric out of the euro zone has become increasingly hostile toward a strong euro, U.S. officials have been generally complacent.  \par \par \uc2 Although the Treasury Department is responsible for dollar policy, Mr. Bernanke's remarks offered further evidence that the dollar's decline isn't sparking any alarm among policy makers. They were also generally in line with attempts by Treasury Secretary John Snow to downplay the dollar's decline by stressing market-based exchange rates while reiterating the "strong dollar" policy.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, a weakening dollar serves the Bush administration's needs, acting along with monetary and fiscal stimulus to spur growth. Because of this, many analysts don't expect any change of policy ahead of the November U.S. presidential elections.  \par \par \uc2 "Bernanke's comments definitely confirm the view for most people that U.S. officials are happy with dollar weakness," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS in London.  \par \par \uc2 He describes the official U.S. response to the dollar, especially from Mr. Snow, as "benign neglect," saying officials have generally "been trying to give reassuring noises that the weakness in the dollar isn't going to be an issue for asset markets."  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Bernanke's remarks resonated even more with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors given the meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations in Florida next month. The G7 meeting in Dubai last September was the catalyst for the most recent leg of the dollar's decline and analysts already think comments by policy makers since then mean there's not likely to be new \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy initiatives coming out of the Florida gathering, certainly not from the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 Stephen Jen, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Morgan Stanley in London, said in a research note that he doesn't expect the U.S. to take a strong position at the G7 gathering. However, Japan is likely to remain strongly against yen strength, and that will put the euro zone "in a difficult situation" but result in no new G7 policy.  \par \par \uc2 So far, the dollar's decline hasn't spilled over into U.S. equity and bond markets and risked sparking a financial market crisis. Several analysts agree with Mr. Bernanke that it probably won't do so either.  \par \par \uc2 UBS, in revising up its year-end 2004 forecast for the euro to $1.40 from $1.32, noted that circumstances are "eerily reminiscent of 1987" when a falling dollar and rising long-term interest rates amid a strong U.S. economy caused stocks to crash.  \par \par \uc2 However, Mohi-uddin reckons the similarities end with the fact that the economy was growing despite a falling dollar. The biggest risk that could send the dollar into a free fall is that Asian central banks stop buying U.S. fixed income as part of their bid to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too quickly, he said. They seem unlikely to pull their support "anytime soon."  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this article.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040105e0150003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
