\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan 5  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2004\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's economy is finally emerging from its prolonged recession, Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said Monday, pointing to signs of improvement in corporate profits and investment appetite as well as progress in clearing bad loans from the banking sector. ("Japan Koizumi," first take ran at 0129 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Vietnam won't encourage domestic firms to issue dollar bonds, but it is considering a sovereign global bond issue to set a benchmark for the international borrowings of domestic firms, Finance Minister Nguyen Sinh Hung told Dow Jones Newswires. ("=INTERVIEW: Vietnam Mulls Global Bond But In No Rush," ran at 0756 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Recent comments from U.S. policy-makers suggest a level of comfort with further dollar weakness, which if it materializes could aid President George W. Bush's re-election bid later this year. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia:Fed, Tsy Appear To Green Light More Dlr Weakness," ran at 0403 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: Steel was hot in China in 2003, attracting billions of dollars of investment and growing attention from fund managers. But China's steel industry, the world's biggest, might begin to face some cold realities this year. ("=CHINA WATCH:Top Steelmakers Stand To Thrive Despite Glut," ran at 0050 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong, which has always prided itself on its business-minded approach to public policy, has now taken that a step further by publishing a set of government finances using the same accrual accounting as private companies. (Story will run by around 0920 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTIONS VIEWS: As the new year gets underway, traders are facing a familiar menace in the options market: the Bank of Japan. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has tended to jump into the market every time things start to take off - like now - stabilizing the dollar and crippling the would-be rally in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  options. There are a few good reasons to think that things might play out differently this time around, however. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views: USD/JPY Vol Could Soar In Coming Weeks," ran at 0320 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's consumer price index fell 0.06% on year in December, its seventh consecutive month of decline, the island's official statistics agency said Monday. A continuing fall in home rentals as well as declining purchases of personal computers and household durable goods was behind the drop, according to Taiwan's Directorate General of Budget, Accounting and Statistics. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan CPI Falls For 7th Straight Month In Dec," ran at 0820 GMT; DATA VIEW will run by around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: A senior central banker in China predicted the recent acceleration in money supply and credit growth will slow down within two years, signaling official confidence in policy-makers' ability to achieve a soft economic landing. ("China/M2," first take ran at 0230 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: A government-backed think tank estimated China's economy grew about 8.9% in 2003, its fastest pace in six years, despite a slowdown in the second quarter because of SARS. ("China Think Tank: '03 GDP Grew At 6-Yr High Of About 8.9%," ran at 0425 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT PIPELINE: It has been a busy start to the Asian debt pipeline year, if not with hard-and-fast deals, then certainly with rumors. Requests for proposals have been dotting the syndicate landscape and talk has also escalated about potential deals from Malaysia, the Philippines and a welter of Korean names. (=ASIA DEBT PIPELINE will run by around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore will unveil a consultation paper on privately managed pension plans Tuesday, in a move that could exclude several fund managers from accessing the island's pool of retirement savings and put downward pressure on management fees. (Story will run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =INDIA DEBT WATCH: A steep three-year fall in India's mortgage rates appears nearly over but this is unlikely to slow hectic demand for housing credit as long as homes remain in short supply, analysts said. ("=INDIA DEBT WATCH: Rates Bottom Won't End Mortgage Boom," ran at 0703 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: South Korea's Woori Bank is seeking to issue a subordinated bond of up to $500 million and is expected to nominate the lead managers in the next few days, people close to the deal said Monday. Although terms of the deal have not been fixed, the bank is looking for a 10-year maturity with a call after five years. ("=EXCLUSIVE:Woori Bk," first take ran at 0554 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's consumer price index picked up slightly in December as higher global oil prices pushed up local gasoline and liquefied petroleum gas prices, the Ministry of Commerce said Monday. ("DATA SNAP: Thai Dec CPI Up On Gasoline, Gas Prices," ran at 0739 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The credit risk for South Korean domestic loans is expected to remain relatively high during the January-March period, particularly for retail loans, according to a survey conducted by the Bank of Korea. ("Bk Of Korea Survey: Credit Risk To Remain High In 1Q 2004," ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar staggered against its major counterparts Monday, Tokyo's first trading day this year, marking a new low against the euro and remaining subdued against the yen despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Weak Vs Majors Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ran at 0532 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's markets brushed off the government's weekend takeover of one of the country's biggest brokers, China Southern Securities Co., sending a strong signal of confidence that the government has the ability to sidestep financial system problems. (WRAP will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's major consumer electronics and mobile-phone handset maker TCL Corp. said Monday it will raise more than 2.51 billion yuan in an initial public offering Wednesday. (WRAP will run by around 1045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Food and beverage maker Tingyi (Cayman Islands) Holding Corp. said Monday it will form a venture with Japanese companies Asahi Breweries Ltd. and Itochu Corp. to expand its business in China. ("HK Tingyi Forms China Drinks Venture With Asahi, Itochu," ran at 0115 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The booming business that Japanese manufacturers of chip-making equipment did late last year might have marked a near-term peak, analysts say. Global orders for Japanese semiconductor-manufacturing equipment soared to a three-year high in November, in what analysts attributed to a rush by companies to boost output capacity ahead of the Athens 2004 Olympic Games beginning from August. ("=Japan Chip Equipment Orders May Have Hit Near-Term Peak," ran at 0622 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's two biggest telecommunications companies said Monday they expect growth in mobile business to boost revenue up to 25% this year. PT Telekomunikasi Indonesia said it foresees a 20% increase in revenue, while rival PT Indonesia Satellite Corp. sees 25% growth. ("=WRAP:Indonesia Telkom, Indosat See Boost From Mobile Ops," ran at 0701 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DRAM REPORT: Spot prices of dynamic random access memory chips are near bottom and likely to rebound as computer retailers begin to add more memory per PC, analysts say. (=ASIA DRAM REPORT will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: German development-finance group DEG is seeking to partner with a mid-sized Asian commercial bank for a shared investment of up to 25% in southwestern Sichuan province's Nanchong City Commercial Bank. ("=INTERVIEW:DEG Seeks Foreign Partners For China Bk Invest," ran at 0822 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: U.S. auto giant General Motors Corp. said Monday it sold a record 386,710 vehicles in China last year, up 46.4% on year, thanks to the launch of new models. ("GM/China Sales," first take ran at 0343 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KARACHI: A buoyant stock market and the roaring success of initial public offerings last year will likely make 2004 another strong year for new listings in Pakistan, analysts said. ("=Pakistan Poised For More IPOs in 2004 On Strong Stk Mkt," ran at 0628 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shoemaker Yue Yuen Industrial (Holdings) Ltd. said Sunday it will realize a profit of about US$23 million by selling 44.8 million shares of its controlling shareholder Pou Chen Corp. ("Yue Yuen Sells 44.8 Mln Pou Chen Shrs; To Gain US$23 Mln," ran at 0122 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: DBS Bank began taking orders Monday for its structured warrants based on the Straits Times Index. Each warrant will be priced at 0.0156% of the index's closing level Monday, with the strike price at 90% of the closing level, according to an issue document seen by Dow Jones Newswires. ("Singapore's DBS/Warrants," first take ran at 0307 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    MELBOURNE: Gas supplies are slowly resuming to manufacturers across two Australian states, as energy company Santos Ltd. warned on Monday that last week's fire at its Moomba gas plant could slice more than 10% from this year's profit. Santos, the majority shareholder of the Moomba facility, which processes gas from Australia's largest onshore gas field, said the plant could be operating at a reduced capacity for at least two months. ("=WRAP: Australian Gas Squeeze Eases, Santos Profit Hit," ran at 0717 GMT) \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040105e0150009d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
