\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Bernanke Weighs In On Dollar, Downplays Risk  \b0\par\par\uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2004\par 20:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Barkley \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--If a recovery in the dollar was part of anyone's New Year's wish list, hopes of a turn in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fortunes were quashed over the weekend by comments from Federal Reserve Governor Ben Bernanke.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Making a somewhat rare foray into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in response to economists' questions in San Diego, Bernanke said the risk of a dollar crisis is "quite low," though not zero.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the euro's nearly 20% rise against the dollar in 2003, the trade-weighted U.S. dollar index remains above the historical average, noted Bernanke. He also said it would be in the interests of U.S. trading partners to avoid a crisis.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, which had enjoyed a brief reprieve Friday from the heavy selling pressure of recent months, was sold off sharply again early Monday, with traders citing Bernanke's comments about both the dollar and his expectations that interest rates could remain at four-decade lows as factors that pushed the euro to yet another record high.  \par \par \uc2 "It gives traders the green light to keep selling the dollar," said Omer Esiner, market analyst at Ruesch International in Washington. "(Bernanke) reiterated the market's outlook, not only for steady interest rates out of the U.S., but also the fact that U.S. officials are more amenable to an orderly dollar decline."  \par \par \uc2 The euro jumped over a cent in Tokyo trade to an all-time high around $1.2694. The dollar hit multiyear lows against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well, including its weakest level in three years against the yen around Y106.10 despite widely suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 Right now, many market participants believe only official action by monetary authorities - or at least strong "verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " - can slow the dollar's slide. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has shown it is gearing up for battle against yen strength by bolstering reserve funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But while the rhetoric out of the euro zone has become increasingly hostile toward a strong euro, U.S. officials have been generally complacent.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par       No Alarm Bells \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the Treasury Department is responsible for dollar policy, Bernanke's remarks offered further evidence that the dollar's decline isn't sparking any alarm among policy makers. They were also generally in line with attempts by Treasury Secretary John Snow to downplay the dollar's decline by stressing market-based exchange rates while reiterating the "strong dollar" policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Working out why U.S. monetary authorities aren't raising red flags in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets now isn't difficult. A weakening dollar serves the Bush administration's needs, acting along with monetary and fiscal stimulus to spur growth. Because of this, many analysts don't expect any change of policy ahead of the November U.S. presidential elections.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Bernanke's comments definitely confirm the view for most people that U.S. officials are happy with dollar weakness," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at UBS in London.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He describes the official U.S. response to the dollar, especially from Snow, as "benign neglect," saying officials have generally "been trying to give reassuring noises that the weakness in the dollar isn't going to be an issue for asset markets."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bernanke's remarks resonated even more with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investors given the meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations in Florida next month. The G7 meeting in Dubai last September was the catalyst for the most recent leg of the dollar's decline and analysts already think comments by policy makers since then mean there's not likely to be new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy initiatives coming out of the Florida gathering, certainly not from the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stephen Jen, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  economist at Morgan Stanley in London, said in a research note that he doesn't expect the U.S. to take a strong position at the G7 gathering. However, Japan is likely to remain strongly against yen strength, and that will put the euro zone "in a difficult situation" but result in no new G7 policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far, the dollar's decline hasn't spilled over into U.S. equity and bond markets and risked sparking a financial market crisis. Several analysts agree with Bernanke that it probably won't do so either.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS, in revising up its year-end 2004 forecast for the euro to $1.40 from $1.32, noted that circumstances are "eerily reminiscent of 1987" when a falling dollar and rising long-term interest rates amid a strong U.S. economy caused stocks to crash.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Mohi-uddin reckons the similarities end with the fact that the economy was growing despite a falling dollar. The biggest risk that could send the dollar into a freefall is that Asian central banks stop buying U.S. fixed income as part of their bid to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from strengthening too quickly, he said. They seem unlikely to pull their support "anytime soon."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, he expects the dollar to eventually stabilize as U.S. interest rates rise and the current account deficit declines.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Tom Barkley is a special writer covering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in New York.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 tom.barkley@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040105e01500042\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
