\par\par \b \uc2 DOLLAR OUTLOOK: No End In Sight To Greenback's Downtrend  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2004\par 20:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Those hoping the dollar might start 2004 on a bright note were disappointed to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sinking further on the first global trading session of the year, foreshadowing a gloomy month and quarter for the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 Coming under renewed pressure after Federal Reserve Governor Ben Bernanke Sunday dismissed concerns over the dollar's accelerating slide lower, the greenback fell sharply in Tokyo and London hours Monday to a new low against the euro and a fresh three-year low against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 But there few tears are likely to have been shed in the analyst community at the dollar's new year but familiar woes. That's because few among them expected anything other than "more of the same".  \par \par \uc2 According to a Dow Jones Newswires survey of six \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts, the euro will rise to $1.2870 in three months. Monday, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a lifetime high of $1.2697.  \par \par \uc2 The average forecast from the same analysts for a one-month outlook suggests the dollar's downward spiral will barely slow down at all in the coming weeks, with the euro seen at $1.2690.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar's prospects look equally bleak, despite the efforts from Japanese monetary authorities to keep the yen at a relatively competitive weak rate for the country's exporters. Indeed, after having sold a record Y20.05 trillion last year in trying to prop up the dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Monday, buying an estimated $5 billion or more, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 According to the average forecast from the six banks surveyed, the dollar will be trading at Y106.10 at the end of this month and at Y103.10 at the end of the quarter. At noon Monday in New York it was at Y106.10, a 40-month low.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar starts 2004 on a very weak footing and we think that little is likely to change as the year progresses," wrote Steve Barrow, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London, in a research note Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Barrow wrote a day after Bernanke, in remarks similar in content and tone to those made by Treasury Secretary John Snow in mid-December, said that the dollar is still high by historical standards. Bernanke also said the chances of a dollar crisis are "quite low" and that interest rates will likely be held down for some time.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight interest rates in the U.S. are 1%, their lowest levels in over 45 years. This relatively poor rate of return is forcing investors to look elsewhere for yield, thus reducing overall portfolio inflows into the U.S.. This has the knock-on effect of highlighting concerns that the U.S. current account deficit will not be funded, which in turn encourages even heavier dollar selling.  \par \par \uc2 One of the few course of action that might stem this vicious spiral lower is a tightening of monetary policy. But as Bernanke indicated, that's not going to happen for some time, certainly not in the first quarter, according to most analysts.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, some think the Fed will stay on hold for the rest of the year, which will keep the dollar under downward pressure going into 2005.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at UBS, one of the biggest participants in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Monday raised their end-2004 euro forecast to $1.40 from $1.32. Analysts at JP Morgan are currently reviewing their near- and long-term forecasts, with the bias of risk pointing to a lower dollar, said Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But as Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York points out, the Fed isn't in charge of dollar policy. That responsibility lies with the Treasury Department.  \par \par \uc2 Until there is some sort of policy guidance from the Treasury or European officials, the slump in the dollar will probably continue.  \par \par \uc2 "How much is too much?" Woolfolk asks. "It's the Treasury's responsibility to provide market guidance," he said, adding that he expects "some verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ahead of the Group of Seven finance ministers in Florida early next month.  \par \par \uc2 That could be the difference between the euro at $1.30 or $1.20 by then, Woolfolk predicted.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                Table Of Banks' Forecasts \par           EUR/USD   1-Mo    3-Mo    USD/JPY   1-Mo   3-Mo \par  \par Bank of New York    1.30    1.35              105    100 \par Bear Stearns        1.25    1.30              107    100 \par Citibank            1.26    1.27              107    107 \par Deutsche Bank       1.2650  1.30              105    103 \par JP Morgan           N/A     1.25              N/A    102 \par UBS                 1.27    1.30              106.50 106.50 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires, 201 938 2096;   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corrected January 5, 2004 14:22 ET (19:22 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, after having sold a record Y20.05 trillion last year in trying to prop up the dollar, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Monday, buying an estimated $5 billion or more, dealers said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (In the "DOLLAR OUTLOOK" which ran at 2:00 p.m. EST (1900 GMT), the amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  estimated in Monday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was incorrectly stated.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1070  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040105e0150003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
