\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Loses Ground to Pound, Franc; Euro Sets Fresh High  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2003\par 21:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar ended a tough year on a down note Wednesday, after a late-day attempt to garner some New Year's Eve cheer sputtered.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese markets were closed for the year-end holiday, but the dollar fell sharply during a fairly active London trading session, ending lower against most of its major rivals and many second-tier ones.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Along with falling to yet another all-time low against the euro at $1.2650, the dollar hit an 11-year bottom against sterling and a seven-year trough against the Swiss franc. In addition, the dollar fell to another 10-year low   against the Canadian dollar and fresh six-year lows against the Australian and New Zealand dollars.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The only sustained gains came against the yen, with Japanese monetary officials widely suspected of buying dollars to keep a strong local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from jeopardizing the country's export-led recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several traders said the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was defending the 106.90-yen level, as apparent stop-loss orders lying below there could trigger a selloff in the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a modest recovery back above 107 yen, however, the move was later accelerated as several U.S. banks were seen buying dollars as part of some year-end bookkeeping. In addition, "some speculative money is still trying to make a buck," said Joseph Barnea, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Bank Leumi in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That rebound did spread to other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for a spell - illiquid markets helped produce intraday highs against the euro and Swiss franc - but it quickly faltered, leaving the dollar lower on the day against most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday afternoon, the euro was trading around $1.2570, up from $1.2552 late Tuesday in New York. It was also at 134.98 yen, up from 134.36 yen. The dollar rose to 107.39 yen from 107.05 yen late Tuesday in New York. Against the Swiss franc, it was at 1.2412 francs, below 1.2435 francs. Sterling was higher at $1.7832 from $1.7775.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets continue to largely ignore positive economic data out of the U.S., including an unexpected drop of 15,000 in weekly initial claims for unemployment benefits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's weakening trend is expected to continue into 2004, with the massive U.S. current account deficit and unattractively low interest rates weighing on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Recent events, including the elevated terrorist-alert level and at least one case of mad-cow disease also have added to the bearish tone for the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even with this retracement, the dollar is still ending on a very weak note, and there are many reasons for that," said Andrew Busch, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Bank of Montreal in Chicago.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro has hit new records in 19 of the past 23 trading days. And after gaining about 20% in 2003, most of the large banks active in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets say the euro will continue to rally, albeit at a slower pace.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Political reasons are partly behind this view: heading into a presidential election year, few analysts think the Bush administration, mindful of the help the economy's getting from a weaker dollar, is about to seek to turn this bearish tide. "There's still a lot of interest to keep the dollar weak, so it may stay that way until November," said Mr. Barnea of Bank Leumi.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the dollar's slide gaining pace, however - and the underlying threat that it could at some point greatly undermine investment in U.S. assets - the question of just how long policy makers can remain complacent in 2004 looms large.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the short term, the dollar may get a reprieve simply because it has fallen so far in such a short period of time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Usually moves this big have some sort of correction," said Mr. Barnea. The euro could be poised for a move back to around $1.22 or $1.2150, he added, and any correction will likely occur by mid-January.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this story.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031231dzcv0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
