\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Markets Wind Down For Year  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,217 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 2003\par 06:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Stocks Stage Last-Hour About-Face \par  \par DJIA          10425.84   loss 24.96     dn 0.2% \par NASDAQ        2009.88    gain 3.40      up 0.2% \par S&P 500       1109.64    gain 0.16         0.9% \par Dow Future    10409.00   gain 9.00      up 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1474.50         0.00         0.0% \par S&P Future    1108.50    gain 0.75      up 0.1% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.27% up 0.02 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Dow Jones industrial average pulled back modestly Tuesday, although most stocks finished higher. Major indexes like the Nasdaq and the S&P 500 are now perched at their highest levels since early 2002. Treasurys ended slightly lower.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Broader-market measures turned higher during the final half hour after trading lower most of the day.  \par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq composite index rose 3.40, or 0.2%, to close at 2,009.88, stretching this year's gain to 51%. The index rose 33 points on Monday to close above 2,000 for the first time since Jan. 15, 2002. The Standard & Poor's 500 index rose 0.16, or 0.01%, to 1,109.64, up 26% for 2003.  \par \par \uc2 While nearly every report in recent months points to a continuing economic recovery in the new year, disappointing job growth has generated worries that the rebound may run out of steam.  \par \par \uc2 Notably, the Conference Board reported Tuesday that its consumer confidence index slipped in December amid persistent anxiety about the job market. After surging in November to its highest level in more than a year, a pullback in the index had been expected. But the numbers released Tuesday were below expectations.  \par \par \uc2 In a separate report Tuesday, the National Association of Realtors reported sales of previously owned homes declined by 4.6% in November. Still, the level of overall sales was strong enough to be the fifth best month on record, a sign that the housing market remains in good shape.  \par \par \uc2 Shares of Rambus Inc. rose 12% on speculation that the computer-memory-chip maker would reach a legal settlement with chip maker Micron Technology Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: U.S. Treasurys ended mostly lower in longer-dated maturities Tuesday, as the data on manufacturing, consumer confidence and housing showed that the economic recovery is broadly still on track.  \par \par \uc2 "While the numbers were a little bit disappointing, they weren't disastrously weak," said Mary Ann Hurley, vice president of fixed-income trading at D.A. Davidson in Seattle.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Chicago Purchasing Managers index for December fell to 59.2 from 64.1 in November. The reading was well under the 62.2 mark economists expected. It is likely to cause some to worry that Friday's release of the December Institute for Supply Management index, a key barometer for the overall economy, may be weaker than currently predicted.  \par \par \uc2 The lone bright spot in Tuesday's data stream was an upbeat report of holiday spending during the Christmas week, which allowed many retailers to reach revenue targets for December after a sluggish start.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Hong Kong Shares Mount Modest Gains \par  \par USD-Yen             106.91       loss  0.14    dn 0.1% \par AUD-USD             0.7498       gain  0.0010  up 0.1% \par Nikkei 225          Closed \par Hang Seng           12575.94     gain  49.20   up 0.4% \par JGB Yield           Closed \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--With Japan's stock market closed for a holiday, Hong Kong took the leadership baton and the Hang Seng Index rose modestly. The HSI has gained around 35% for the year, roaring back from April's SARS scare.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers said investors are confident shares will perform well into the first half of 2004, backed by continuing economic expansion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold an additional HK$707 million into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets late Tuesday to prevent the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from strengthening further against the U.S. dollar. Added to the HK$520 million the HKMA sold Tuesday afternoon, the day's total comes to HK$1.23 billion, equivalent to buying of about US$158 million.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, Indian pharmaceutical maker Dr. Reddy's Laboratories Ltd. said GlaxoSmithKline Plc filed a lawsuit against it in the U.S. for alleged patent infringement of a migraine drug.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Euro Rises In Thin Markets \par  \par Euro-USD            1.2566      gain  0.0020  up 0.2% \par Stlg-USD            1.7800      gain  0.0040  up 0.2% \par USD-Franc           1.2394      loss  0.0036  dn 0.3% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European stocks and government bonds are set for a quiet and trendless session, although the euro is still flexing its muscles.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares may open little changed or a shade higher - largely in technology names - amid very light volumes. Many markets are closed, or trading in the morning only.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks were higher Tuesday but lackluster U.S. economic data caused indexes to close off the highs of the session.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts at Barclays Stockbrokers said despite a weak U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , heightened security fears, health scares, natural disasters and the ongoing Parmalat debacle, the equity market's momentum remains intact.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technology stocks were the session outperformers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Government bond prices are likely to open little changed or slightly higher in some maturities as the dollar continues to slide against European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone government bonds and gilts fell Tuesday in tracking Treasurys' earlier losses.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, some doubt remains about whether a stronger euro is enough to prop bonds up.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cyril Beuzit, global head of interest rate strategy at BNP Paribas, said, "The only supporting factor (for eurobond markets) is obviously the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is relatively well supported recently, but when you look at the upcoming economic data our view is that we should continue to sell the market on rallies."  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro zeroed in on $1.2600 overnight, continuing its blazing flight higher. But market participants became somewhat wary of overdoing the push, out of fear that central bankers might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In 2003, the dollar has plummeted about 20% against the euro, with a loss of roughly five of those percentage points coming in December. Against the yen, the dollar has dropped around 11% in the past 12 months, slowed by $200 billion worth of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Higher interest rates may be the dollar's salvation, but for the moment, this scenario appears to be at the outer edges of the radar screen," noted Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of New York. "Hence, it is (unnervingly) difficult to escape the conclusion that there is still plenty of downside to the dollar ahead of us."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Wednesday, Dec 31: French, U.S. Jobless \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0245/0745 France   November Unemployment Rate (seen 9.7%) \par 0700/1200 U.S.     MBA Refinancing Index (last -7.9%) \par 0830/1330 U.S.     Initial Jobless Claims for December 27 week \par 1000/1500 U.S.     DJ-BTM Business Barometer for December 20 \par 1400/1900 U.S.     Bond market closes early \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181; dennis.baker@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an item timed at around 0559 GMT, the S&P 500 percentage change in the chart was misstated: it should read 0.0%, not 0.9%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1011  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031231dzcv00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
