\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-G7 to discuss dollar slide as Europe frets-source.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Guido Bohsem  \par \uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2003\par 15:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BERLIN, Dec 30 (Reuters) - Finance ministers from the Group of Seven richest nations will discuss the dollar's slide when they meet in February, as Europeans become increasingly worried it may hurt economic recovery, a G7 source told Reuters on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 A euro level clearly higher than $1.20 could not be good for Europe's economy, while a level of $1.30 represented a "pain barrier", the source said.  \par \par \uc2 A stronger single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  , which broke the $1.25 barrier for the first time on Monday, can erode the euro zone's competitiveness by making goods produced there more expensive to customers outside the 12-nation region.  \par \par \uc2 But the source said the United States should also not be happy with a sharply weaker dollar which could lead to a flight of capital from the country, depressing stock markets and pushing up long-term interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers begin a two-day meeting in Boca Raton, Florida, on February 6, their first since gathering in Dubai last September when they called for flexible exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Although their Dubai statement was aimed at Asian countries, it chiefly accelerated the dollar's slide against the euro and other leading free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside Asia.  \par \par \uc2 One euro zone monetary source told Reuters that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be on the agenda at the next G7 meeting and policymakers needed to avoid confusion caused by the Dubai statement.  \par \par \uc2 Europe prefers to coordinate policies with its G7 allies, but the source did not rule out euro zone officials acting alone if euro appreciation threatened to destabilise markets or an economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell slightly in reaction to the G7 source's comment but quickly recovered to stand just below this week's record high around $1.2511.  \par \par \uc2 "It's probably just another German moaning which is why the market isn't paying much attention," said Julian Jessop, chief European economist at Standard Chartered in London.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no doubt that some European politicians and central bankers are concerned about the euro's strength, but I doubt the U.S. will care what the Europeans say. The Germans might want to discuss the euro but it is unlikely the Americans will."  \par \par \uc2 OFFICIAL CALM  \par \par \uc2 The euro's value has risen nearly 20 percent this year. The German government said on Monday it was relaxed about the level of the euro, adding German exporters were extremely competitive.  \par \par \uc2 Juergen Stark, Vice President of the Bundesbank, agreed the euro's value was no cause for worry, telling Germany's Boersen Zeitung newspaper on Tuesday the balance between good and bad effects from its appreciation was "not necessarily negative".  \par \par \uc2 Only 10 percent of German exports went to the United States, compared with about 50 percent within the euro zone, he said.  \par \par \uc2 German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder said in an article in Tuesday's Handelsblatt business daily that German exports appear to be strengthening despite the euro's climb.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, figures released on Tuesday showed Germany's trade surplus widened to a record in 2003 as Europe's biggest economy, exported more goods than ever before.  \par \par \uc2 Behind the official calm, however, there are signs of growing unease. A senior European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker was quoted on Monday as saying the ECB was growing increasingly uneasy that euro strength could undermine recovery in the bloc.  \par \par \uc2 Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London, highlighted the discrepancy between comment from the G7 sources and those on the record who formulate policy.  \par \par \uc2 "What you need is for one of them to say something as dramatic as the sources - and then you probably will see a reaction in the market."  \par \par \uc2 Dealers suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Monday and expected further action if the dollar fell towards 106.74 yen, the three-year low hit on December 9.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was trading close to this week's 11-year high against the dollar on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Charlotte Cooper in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031230dzcu003bm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
