\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar struggles just above record lows vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2003\par 12:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 30 (Reuters) - The dollar wobbled just above the previous session's record low against the euro on Tuesday as euro zone policy officials appeared relaxed about a strong single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and U.S. structural concerns persisted.  \par \par \uc2 The tone of euro zone officials was likely to come under increased scrutiny in the lead up to a Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting in early February, as markets look for any signs of concern about the dollar's decline from the G7.  \par \par \uc2 But so far, European officials have shown little alarm about the euro's rise of nearly 20 percent against the dollar this year, driven largely by worries over the U.S. current account deficit. German manufacturers, who stand to lose the most from a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also remained optimistic.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday a survey of German firms showed them in buoyant mood and Germany's government said it was not worried.  \par \par \uc2 "The German comments were a positive for the euro and this will remain the main theme now. Markets will be looking for concern from euro zone officials about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the G7," said Michael Metcalfe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at State Street in London.  \par \par \uc2 "The ECB's line still is that it is difficult to justify being concerned."  \par \par \uc2 At 1100 GMT, the euro traded around $1.25, slightly up on the day and just below record highs of $1.2511. The dollar was also near a seven-year low on the Swiss franc and a three-year low on the yen.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. ECONOMIC GLITCHES  \par \par \uc2 A series of pieces of economic data from the United States was high on the market's agenda, including consumer confidence, existing home sales and numbers on U.S. Midwest manufacturing, all due at 1500 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The numbers were seen in line with a recent slowdown in improving indicators.  \par \par \uc2 The Conference Board's index of consumer confidence was seen falling to 91.5 for December from the previous month's 91.7, following a dip in this month's consumer sentiment measure calculated by the University of Michigan.  \par \par \uc2 Existing home sales were seen down slightly to 6.3 million in November from the previous month's 6.35 million. New home sales have already shown an unexpected decline in figures released last week.  \par \par \uc2 The December National Association of Purchasing Management-Chicago business barometer was expected to fall to 62.2 from 64.10 in November.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts said bad or good data alike was likely to add to the dollar's downtrend.  \par \par \uc2 While strong U.S. numbers boost global recovery optimism, encouraging money to flow into higher growth regions such as Asia, weak figures reinforce expectations U.S. interest rates will remain low, keeping dollar deposits unattractive for foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 "At the root of the dollar's weakness is the fact that global capital is not flowing back to the United States," said Kota Kimura, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 JAPANESE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The dollar's broad weakness was again fuelling talk of yen-selling by Japan, which is concerned that a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could pull the rug from underneath the fragile, export-driven economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounted to 2.25 trillion yen ($21.04 billion) in the month ending on December 26. This meant Japan has sold roughly 20 trillion yen so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers suspected more yen selling on Monday and expected possible further sales should the dollar fall much further towards 106.75, a three-year low hit on December 9.  \par \par \uc2 "This is a record year for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the market is continuously surprised by the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But that said, it is only a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said State Street's Metcalfe. "And it will continue."  \par \par \uc2 The yen showed little reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data, holding around 107 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Trading volumes were thin in Tokyo's last trading day of the year. Markets will resume next Monday after New Year holidays.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031230dzcu0036m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
