\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan forex intervention \highlight\uc2  record Y20 trln in 2003.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano   \par \uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2003\par 11:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record 20 trillion yen ($187 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year as it sought to curb further strengthening of the yen against the free-falling U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday it sold 2.2519 trillion yen in the period from November 27 to December 26, during which the dollar hit a three-year low of 106.74 yen .   \par \par \uc2 The figure, which was a little larger than market expectations, came on top of 17.8 trillion yen Tokyo used in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to November.   \par \par \uc2 "It was a bit larger than expected, although it was hardly a surprise either," said Junya Tanase, a strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2 The heavy yen selling reflects worries of Japanese officials that a strong yen could stifle the nation's export-led recovery and weaken domestic share prices, which are key to the health of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking system.   \par \par \uc2 The aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year came as the dollar relentlessly fell against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 A wide range of worries - from the yawning U.S. current account deficit to concerns of extremist attacks - have prompted global investors to flee U.S. shares and bonds.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen more than 15 percent against the euro this year and hit a record low of $1.2510 per euro on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 The greenback has also fallen about 10 percent versus the yen. It was trading just under 107 yen on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 REPLENISHING WAR CHEST   \par \par \uc2 Many market players expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue to actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market next year after the government earlier this month took several steps to ensure it has enough ammunition for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 To finance its dollar buying the government borrows yen by selling financing bills, within a borrowing limit approved by parliament.   \par \par \uc2 Some market players had speculated that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing may have neared its limit, possibly with only a few trillion yen left in the coffers, due to this year's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 To quell such speculation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier this month it would seek to raise the borrowing limit to 140 trillion yen in the fiscal year starting next April from the current 79 trillion yen.   \par \par \uc2 The massive increase of 61 trillion yen, equivalent to about $570 billion, roughly matches the annual U.S. current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry also recently made an arrangement with the Bank of Japan in which it can raise funds by selling foreign bonds to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a stopgap measure to avoid a funding crunch before parliament approves the raising of the borrowing limit.   \par \par \uc2 By buying U.S. Treasury debt with the dollars obtained from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not only financing the U.S. current account deficit but is also help financing the U.S. budget deficit. "The situation in which the Japanese authorities are financing a considerable part of the U.S. budget and current account deficits appears likely to continue well into 2004," said Hidenori Suezawa, chief strategist at Daiwa Securities SMBC.   \par \par \uc2 But while Japanese authorities, along with some other Asian central banks, appear content to finance the U.S. twin deficits, many private investors are shying away from dollar-based assets, leaving the dollar vulnerable.   \par \par \uc2 Some market players expect the dollar to continue to fall early next year, possibly testing below 100 yen. ($1=106.94 Yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031230dzcu00317\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
