\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar under fire as new record lows beckon.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2003\par 10:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 30 (Reuters) - The dollar remained under fire on Tuesday, teetering near the previous day's record low against the euro as pressure from low dollar yields and U.S. current account deficit worries showed no sign of abating.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, whose value against the dollar has risen nearly 20 percent this year, was also supported by a comment on Monday by the German government that it was not worried about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength and there was no reason to consider taking measures to counter it.  \par \par \uc2 "Germany is comfortable with the euro's grind higher and there is nothing yet to cause a turn in the trend," said Trevor Dinmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank in London.  \par \par \uc2 The euro hovered around $1.2500 at 0845 GMT, having reached an all-time high of $1.2511 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. consumer confidence, existing home sales and Midwest manufacturing data are due later in the session, but few expect much reprieve for the dollar which has recently fallen on both good and poor economic news.  \par \par \uc2 While strong U.S. numbers boost global recovery optimism, encouraging money to flow into higher growth regions such as Asia, weak figures reinforce expectations U.S. interest rates will remain low, keeping dollar deposits unattractive for foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 "At the root of the dollar's weakness is the fact that global capital is not flowing back to the United States," said Kota Kimura, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 JAPANESE TACTICS  \par \par \uc2 The dollar teetered around 106.90 yen with dealers wary of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the dollar fall much further.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday and expected further action if the dollar falls towards 106.74, the three-year low hit on December 9.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials worry that a strong yen hurts exports, which are driving an economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japan threw a record 17.8 trillion yen at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the first 11 months of this year in a bid to retain export competitiveness and will release \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures for the four weeks to last Friday at 1000 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the data to show \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily at least twice during that period - on December 9, when the dollar hit its three-year low, and on December 24, when it again fell below 107 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Junya Tanase, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan, said he expected the December figure to be between one and two trillion yen ($9.3-18.7 billion).  \par \par \uc2 Trading volumes were thin in Tokyo's last trading day of the year. Markets will resume next Monday after New Year holidays.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031230dzcu002v3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
