\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar a whisker away from record low vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2003\par 07:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 30 (Reuters) - The dollar dipped on Tuesday, threatening to hit another record low versus the euro, on a mixture of weak factors, including security concerns, a widening U.S. deficit and low dollar yields.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, whose value against the dollar has risen nearly 20 percent this year, was also helped by comments by the German government that it is not worried about euro strength and there is no reason to consider taking measures to try to counter it.  \par \par \uc2 "At the root of the dollar's weakness is the fact that global capital is not flowing back to the United States," said Kota Kimura, assistant \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 As of 0600 GMT, the euro was at $1.2500/03, less than 0.1 percent away from its all-time high of around $1.2510 hit in Europe on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "There is absolutely no change in the trend," said Kenji Kobayashi, senior manager at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division of Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 Following its recent pattern, the dollar failed to capitalise on Monday on a rally in the U.S. Nasdaq stock index to its highest level in about two years.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been susceptible to weak U.S. economic data, making dollar holders nervous ahead of U.S. data later in the day that includes consumer confidence, Midwest manufacturing and existing home sales.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar tends to slip on weak U.S. data so caution is needed," said Junya Tanase, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 The Conference Board index of consumer confidence for December is expected to show a fall to 91.5, while the National Association of Purchasing Management-Chicago business barometer is seen inching down to 62.2. November existing home sales are expected to have slipped a bit to 6.30 million.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar's weakness was countered by wariness over possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 It stayed little changed from late U.S levels around 107.00 yen Trading was limited on the last trading day of the year in Tokyo. Markets will resume next Monday after New Year holidays.  \par \par \uc2 Still, many Japanese exporters are leaving dollar sell orders at around 108 yen so as not to miss any opportunity to sell dollars they earn overseas during the holidays. These orders are likely to limit the dollar's rise, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers also suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday when the dollar fell to 106.90 yen, and they said further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely if the dollar falls towards a three-year low of 106.74 yen hit on December 9. Japanese officials worry that a strong yen hurts exports, which are driving an economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "They will definitely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mitsuo Imaizumi, deputy general manager of the international bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  department at Daiwa Securities SMBC.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures at 7 p.m. (1000 GMT) for the four weeks to last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the data to show \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily at least twice during that period - on December 9, when the dollar hit its three-year low, and on December 24, when it again fell below 107 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Tanase of J.P. Morgan said he expected the December figure to be between one and two trillion yen ($9.3-18.7 billion).  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted record 17.8 trillion yen of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in first 11 months of 2003.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031230dzcu002en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
